1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,162
WWW.MY-SUBS.CO

1
00:00:11,520 --> 00:00:13,560
Get that, will you?

2
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:17,080
Anna, what have you got for hair
in the evening?

3
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:19,240
Er, diamond stars and one tiara.

4
00:00:19,240 --> 00:00:22,040
We may not use it,
but I'd rather be safe than sorry.

5
00:00:31,960 --> 00:00:36,560
Wrap it all in brown paper so they
won't have a basket to worry about
at the other end.

6
00:00:38,480 --> 00:00:41,560
Suitcases are finished.
OK. I'll go up and get them now.

7
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:43,600
It's quite a palaver, isn't it?

8
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:45,640
Do they go to Duneagle every year?

9
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,720
Not last year,
after Lady Sybil died.

10
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:49,760
And not during the war.

11
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:53,200
But otherwise, it's the high spot
of His Lordship's calendar.

12
00:00:58,960 --> 00:01:00,600
Come on - quickly, quickly.

13
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,600
Straight on to the wagonette.

14
00:01:06,680 --> 00:01:08,720
Why aren't we taking the guns?

15
00:01:08,720 --> 00:01:11,280
Because you're going stalking,
Mr Molesley,

16
00:01:11,280 --> 00:01:14,240
and stalking
does not involve shotguns.

17
00:01:14,240 --> 00:01:16,560
Maybe we should take them,
just to be sure.

18
00:01:16,560 --> 00:01:18,560
I'm already sure.

19
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:22,120
Yeah.

20
00:01:52,160 --> 00:01:53,880
Why don't I go on my own?

21
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:55,920
Darling, this isn't 1850.

22
00:01:55,920 --> 00:01:59,200
No-one expects me to hide indoors
until the baby's born.

23
00:01:59,200 --> 00:02:01,280
Well, all right. If you're certain.

24
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:07,880
If you change your mind at any point
and want to come home, just tell me.

25
00:02:12,360 --> 00:02:15,240
Well, no.
We'd be thrilled to see you.

26
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:19,800
It just seems an awfully long way
to come for a walk.

27
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:23,200
Who was it?

28
00:02:23,200 --> 00:02:25,520
My editor, Michael Gregson.

29
00:02:27,280 --> 00:02:30,280
He's realised he'll be in Scotland
at the same time as us.

30
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:33,400
Don't tell me
he'll be near Duneagle. Apparently.

31
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:35,160
What a coincidence.

32
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:37,280
Yes, isn't it?

33
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:40,120
Ask him over. We want to meet him,
don't we, Robert?

34
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:43,560
Why are the Flintshires based
in Scotland when the title's Welsh?

35
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:47,240
Shrimpie's grandmother was Countess
of Newtonmoor in her own right.

36
00:02:47,240 --> 00:02:49,120
It's now their courtesy title.

37
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:51,000
Dare one ask
why he's called Shrimpie?

38
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:53,040
It was a nursery game.

39
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:56,880
Louisa was a lobster, Agatha was
a shark, which is easy to believe,

40
00:02:56,880 --> 00:02:58,920
and I suppose
Shrimpie was a shrimp.

41
00:02:58,920 --> 00:03:01,360
Is he very small?

42
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:03,280
No, but he was the youngest.

43
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:06,440
I'm sorry you won't be with us.

44
00:03:06,440 --> 00:03:09,080
Why should they ask me?
I don't know them at all.

45
00:03:09,080 --> 00:03:10,680
Nor do I, really.

46
00:03:10,680 --> 00:03:13,160
Are you sure you should be going?

47
00:03:13,160 --> 00:03:16,360
Don't be a spoilsport.
I still have a month.

48
00:03:16,360 --> 00:03:19,840
(HARRUMPHS)
You don't want me to go, either?

49
00:03:19,840 --> 00:03:23,840
I think you should take good care
of yourself, milady. That's all.

50
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:27,000
I agree. Right, let's get started.

51
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,680
I told Mama we'd be on the platform
at quarter to, and we're late.

52
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:32,680
(STEAM HISSES)

53
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:36,400
Feels like a holiday, doesn't it?

54
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:39,880
Oh, don't worry. It won't feel
like a holiday once we get there.

55
00:03:39,880 --> 00:03:43,840
Let me just retrieve the briefcase.
They might need it for the journey.

56
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:45,840
(LAUGHS)

57
00:03:46,880 --> 00:03:49,640
I want to check their dining-car
seats for lunch.

58
00:03:49,640 --> 00:03:51,680
Do you like Scotland?

59
00:03:51,680 --> 00:03:54,640
Have you really never been?
They didn't go last year.

60
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:57,720
And you know I wasn't
a proper lady's maid before that.

61
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:00,440
No, I meant as a child,
or when you were growing up.

62
00:04:01,480 --> 00:04:03,520
My mother's mother was Scottish.

63
00:04:03,520 --> 00:04:05,520
She was a Keith -
did I tell you that?

64
00:04:05,520 --> 00:04:06,960
No.

65
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:11,320
Now, you won't forget to take her
for some decent walks?

66
00:04:11,320 --> 00:04:13,120
She can be lazy.

67
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:15,120
Don't worry. She'll be fine.

68
00:04:18,840 --> 00:04:20,320
Have you got everything?

69
00:04:20,320 --> 00:04:22,400
Well, if I haven't,
it's too late now.

70
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:27,080
Do you think it's wise
to leave him here unsupervised?

71
00:04:27,080 --> 00:04:31,000
What do you mean? Well, I know
he's house-broken, more or less,

72
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,320
but I don't want freedom
to go to his head.

73
00:04:33,320 --> 00:04:35,440
I'll keep an eye on him.

74
00:04:35,440 --> 00:04:37,800
He can come to dinner tonight.

75
00:04:37,800 --> 00:04:40,360
Oh.
Well, that's one day taken care of.

76
00:04:40,360 --> 00:04:42,360
Only nine to go!

77
00:04:43,520 --> 00:04:45,400
(STEAM HISSES)

78
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:53,000
(WHISTLE BLOWS)

79
00:05:18,320 --> 00:05:21,440
So will we have a bit of a break
while they're away, Mr Carson?

80
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:23,080
What?

81
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:26,520
He meant can we expect
some time off?

82
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:28,520
For an outing, or something.

83
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:30,000
I don't understand.

84
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:32,280
Has someone forgotten
to pay your wages?

85
00:05:32,280 --> 00:05:34,160
No.

86
00:05:34,160 --> 00:05:35,840
Exactly.

87
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:38,320
Now, we will start
with the ceremonial wear,

88
00:05:38,320 --> 00:05:42,200
and when that's done, I want all the
silver brought down for cleaning -

89
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:45,480
one room at a time.
(THEY SNIGGER)

90
00:05:45,480 --> 00:05:47,800
And don't you maids
think you're out of it.

91
00:05:47,800 --> 00:05:51,360
We'll give every room a thorough
cleaning while they're away.

92
00:05:54,760 --> 00:05:58,200
You can let them have
a bit of free time, can't you?

93
00:05:58,200 --> 00:06:01,960
If they get the extra work done,
then I'll think about it.

94
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:10,240
Edna?

95
00:06:12,640 --> 00:06:16,040
Why hasn't Mr Branson been asked
to go with the others?

96
00:06:17,040 --> 00:06:19,040
I'm sure I don't know.

97
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:24,000
I wonder what Lady Flintshire
made of her cousin's daughter
eloping with a chauffeur.

98
00:06:25,400 --> 00:06:27,800
It's not your place to wonder.

99
00:06:31,440 --> 00:06:33,160
What was she like?

100
00:06:33,160 --> 00:06:35,520
She was a sweet, kind person.

101
00:06:36,520 --> 00:06:38,600
And a real beauty.

102
00:06:38,600 --> 00:06:40,600
Inside and out.

103
00:06:42,640 --> 00:06:45,080
You'd think
she could have done better.

104
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:50,480
But SHE didn't think she could do
better, and that's what matters.

105
00:06:51,520 --> 00:06:53,560
He's nice-looking -
I'll give him that.

106
00:06:54,640 --> 00:06:57,640
I don't think you're required
to give him anything.

107
00:07:01,680 --> 00:07:04,080
She'll be a long way off.

108
00:07:04,080 --> 00:07:06,120
But not in the middle of nowhere.

109
00:07:06,120 --> 00:07:08,360
There are hospitals in Inverness.

110
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:12,280
You mean I'm being an old woman.

111
00:07:12,280 --> 00:07:16,920
Well, after Sybil,
who can blame you?

112
00:07:19,160 --> 00:07:22,880
It'll be very quiet for you,
with them all gone.

113
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:24,880
It will.

114
00:07:24,880 --> 00:07:27,760
Why not come to supper tomorrow?

115
00:07:27,760 --> 00:07:31,080
I daren't call it dinner, as I
don't know what we can conjure up.

116
00:07:31,080 --> 00:07:33,520
But do come.
I don't want to be a nuisance.

117
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:36,160
You won't be.
Tom Branson's here this evening,

118
00:07:36,160 --> 00:07:38,120
so I shall be back in training.

119
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:41,760
Thank you. I'd be delighted.

120
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:49,760
Oh, Mr Carson sent me up to clear.

121
00:07:49,760 --> 00:07:51,800
He thought you'd be out of here.

122
00:07:51,800 --> 00:07:53,800
I should be.

123
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:56,520
Seems sad for you to be left behind
all on your own.

124
00:07:56,520 --> 00:07:58,160
I'm used to it.

125
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:01,720
Yes. Yes, of course. You would be.

126
00:08:04,640 --> 00:08:06,680
It must be very hard.

127
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:08,720
Don't worry about me.

128
00:08:08,720 --> 00:08:10,760
Plenty to do.

129
00:08:10,760 --> 00:08:14,520
You're the new maid, aren't you?
What's your name?

130
00:08:14,520 --> 00:08:16,360
Edna.

131
00:08:16,360 --> 00:08:18,120
Edna Braithwaite.

132
00:08:39,400 --> 00:08:41,280
Hello!
Robert, how are you?

133
00:08:41,280 --> 00:08:43,320
Shrimpie, this is so nice of you.

134
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:45,520
Nonsense. I can't tell you
how glad we are.

135
00:08:45,520 --> 00:08:47,600
Cora. Edith.

136
00:08:47,600 --> 00:08:48,880
Hello!

137
00:08:49,880 --> 00:08:53,520
Daddy, this is Matthew,
defender of the downtrodden -

138
00:08:53,520 --> 00:08:56,320
including me!
I don't know why I've earned that.

139
00:08:56,320 --> 00:08:58,520
We met at your wedding. Mary.

140
00:08:58,520 --> 00:09:03,720
Aunt Violet, we feel so privileged
to have lured you this far north.

141
00:09:03,720 --> 00:09:06,240
You flatter me,
which is just as it should be.

142
00:09:06,240 --> 00:09:07,880
We've got lots planned.

143
00:09:07,880 --> 00:09:10,880
There's the Ghillies' Ball,
which Mary's always the star of.

144
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:12,520
Rose, don't wear them out.

145
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:15,800
We remembered how you liked
our mountain loch,

146
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:19,160
and we've arranged for luncheon
to be taken up there one day.

147
00:09:19,160 --> 00:09:21,160
That was my idea.

148
00:09:22,160 --> 00:09:24,760
Well, whosever idea it was,
it's a lovely one.

149
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:28,240
Tea is in the library
when you're ready to come down.

150
00:09:28,240 --> 00:09:30,240
(EXCITED CHATTER)

151
00:09:57,800 --> 00:10:00,160
I'm looking for a Mrs Patmore.

152
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:03,080
Why is that, then?
I've got some deliveries for her.

153
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:05,720
Really? Where from?

154
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:07,880
You're not very curious,
are you (?)

155
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:10,000
And you're not one of our regulars.

156
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:13,640
Well, if you must know, I've taken
over from Mr Cox in Thirsk.

157
00:10:13,640 --> 00:10:16,520
Mr Patmore sent an order in to him.
What's this?

158
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:18,680
Do I hear my name taken in vain?

159
00:10:18,680 --> 00:10:22,280
I was just explaining.
You sent an order in to Mr Cox.

160
00:10:22,280 --> 00:10:24,760
I've bought the shop
and all the stock,

161
00:10:24,760 --> 00:10:27,400
so I took the liberty
of filling it out.

162
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:30,720
How do you know
she wants to do business with you?

163
00:10:30,720 --> 00:10:34,160
All right, Mr Barrow. I can fight
my own battles, thank you.

164
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:36,160
So where is this order, then?

165
00:10:39,680 --> 00:10:41,680
Bring it to the kitchen.

166
00:10:42,720 --> 00:10:45,080
And what's your name,
since you know mine?

167
00:10:45,080 --> 00:10:47,640
It's Tufton. Joss Tufton.

168
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:55,720
Hmm.

169
00:10:55,720 --> 00:10:57,800
What's this?

170
00:10:57,800 --> 00:11:00,520
Some vichyssoise left over
from last night.

171
00:11:01,720 --> 00:11:03,720
Oh, that's heaven!

172
00:11:04,720 --> 00:11:06,800
Any more leftovers going begging?

173
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:08,800
Have a bit of the tart if you like.

174
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:11,840
I don't mind if I do.

175
00:11:15,680 --> 00:11:19,160
I've not had food that good since
the last time I were in London.

176
00:11:19,160 --> 00:11:20,760
I'm not just a pretty face.

177
00:11:21,800 --> 00:11:24,240
This family's fallen on its feet
and no mistake.

178
00:11:24,240 --> 00:11:26,520
I wouldn't mind eating food like
that every day.

179
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:31,360
Enough of the flannel. I'll keep
the order, but if there's owt amiss
you'll be hearing from me.

180
00:11:33,800 --> 00:11:37,360
Let's just hope that there's
something not quite up to scratch.

181
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:39,000
Why do you say that?

182
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,520
Cos I'd like to hear
from you again, Mrs Patmore.

183
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:43,280
I would.

184
00:11:44,320 --> 00:11:47,200
Be off with you, you cheeky devil!
Go on!

185
00:11:57,362 --> 00:11:59,082
Are you not hungry?

186
00:12:00,122 --> 00:12:03,162
It's a bit early for us. We eat
our dinner after the family's.

187
00:12:03,162 --> 00:12:06,282
Oh, I agree with you, Miss Crawley.

188
00:12:06,282 --> 00:12:09,282
In London we eat last thing,
when all the work is done,

189
00:12:09,282 --> 00:12:11,322
and I prefer it.

190
00:12:11,322 --> 00:12:13,122
How about you, Miss Grantham?

191
00:12:13,122 --> 00:12:15,162
Me?

192
00:12:15,162 --> 00:12:17,562
Oh, I do what I'm told.

193
00:12:17,562 --> 00:12:22,442
It makes me laugh when I hear
Miss O'Brien and Mr Bates
called Mr and Miss Grantham.

194
00:12:24,522 --> 00:12:28,282
Miss Bates and I don't often work
in the same house party.

195
00:12:28,282 --> 00:12:30,922
Of course - you two are married,
Miss Crawley.

196
00:12:30,922 --> 00:12:35,162
How do you manage at home,
being called Bates and Bates?

197
00:12:35,162 --> 00:12:39,882
We're not. They still call me Anna,
like when I was a housemaid.

198
00:12:39,882 --> 00:12:41,922
Which isn't right.

199
00:12:41,922 --> 00:12:44,882
I do so hate
to see a lady's maid downgraded.

200
00:12:44,882 --> 00:12:46,922
Oh, I so agree, Miss Grantham.

201
00:12:46,922 --> 00:12:49,402
But then, we would think alike,
wouldn't we?

202
00:12:49,402 --> 00:12:53,562
It's a treat to have a kindred
spirit come to stay. It really is.

203
00:12:59,122 --> 00:13:01,002
Tell Mrs Crane I've gone up.

204
00:13:01,002 --> 00:13:03,082
I'll announce dinner in 10 minutes.

205
00:13:44,922 --> 00:13:46,562
How marvellous.

206
00:13:46,562 --> 00:13:48,562
I should remind you
that he'll be back

207
00:13:48,562 --> 00:13:50,762
to pipe us awake at eight o'clock.

208
00:13:50,762 --> 00:13:53,482
And he keeps it up
through breakfast.

209
00:13:53,482 --> 00:13:56,762
So the chances of getting back
to sleep again are nil.

210
00:13:56,762 --> 00:13:58,922
All right, Shrimpie,
the point has been made.

211
00:13:58,922 --> 00:14:00,802
You've no need to apologise.

212
00:14:00,802 --> 00:14:03,642
I'm glad to see
the old ways being maintained.

213
00:14:03,642 --> 00:14:06,922
Tomorrow, we'll kit you out
with some rifles for some practice.

214
00:14:06,922 --> 00:14:08,962
And what is planned for the women?

215
00:14:08,962 --> 00:14:11,722
Well, there's a picnic by the loch
the day after tomorrow.

216
00:14:11,722 --> 00:14:14,962
And the Ghillies' Ball on Friday
is always good fun.

217
00:14:14,962 --> 00:14:17,002
As long as it's not too much fun.

218
00:14:17,002 --> 00:14:21,002
As a matter of fact, a friend
of mine is staying quite nearby.

219
00:14:21,002 --> 00:14:23,642
I thought I might telephone him.

220
00:14:23,642 --> 00:14:26,202
But you must ask him here.
She doesn't have to.

221
00:14:26,202 --> 00:14:28,242
Oh, please do.
I'd like to meet him.

222
00:14:28,242 --> 00:14:31,602
That's settled, then.
Invite him to dinner tomorrow night.

223
00:14:31,602 --> 00:14:34,682
Unless, of course, Susan objects.

224
00:14:34,682 --> 00:14:36,722
Why on earth would I object?

225
00:14:39,042 --> 00:14:41,602
I thought it was
too good to be true.

226
00:14:41,602 --> 00:14:43,882
He's sent dried ginger
instead of fresh.

227
00:14:43,882 --> 00:14:48,682
It's my fault. I just put 'ginger'
because Mr Cox knew what I liked.

228
00:14:48,682 --> 00:14:50,722
I might go into Thirsk tomorrow.

229
00:14:50,722 --> 00:14:52,882
I can take it back for you
if you want.

230
00:14:52,882 --> 00:14:57,282
Would you? You should go with him,
Alfred. You'd like that shop.

231
00:14:57,282 --> 00:14:58,722
Who's doing what tomorrow?

232
00:14:58,722 --> 00:15:02,562
Mr Barrow's going in to Thirsk.
I said Alfred should go with him.

233
00:15:02,562 --> 00:15:05,802
Who says I can spare them?
What about Mr Branson's luncheon?

234
00:15:05,802 --> 00:15:08,482
I doubt he needs an under-butler,
Mr Carson.

235
00:15:08,482 --> 00:15:10,522
Or two footmen.

236
00:15:10,522 --> 00:15:13,162
He told me he'd get lunch
at the Grantham Arms.

237
00:15:13,162 --> 00:15:15,202
So can they go?

238
00:15:15,202 --> 00:15:18,842
I suppose so. But there's a lot
to be done, and don't forget it.

239
00:15:19,882 --> 00:15:22,242
I hope you'll come here
whenever you like.

240
00:15:22,242 --> 00:15:25,842
It must be odd, being alone
in that great house.

241
00:15:25,842 --> 00:15:27,162
Well, I'm not alone.

242
00:15:27,162 --> 00:15:29,202
There's people I know well.

243
00:15:29,202 --> 00:15:31,642
Except they're all downstairs,
and I'm up.

244
00:15:31,642 --> 00:15:35,562
Well, why not take the opportunity
to spend some time with them?

245
00:15:35,562 --> 00:15:39,122
I don't think old Lady Grantham
would approve of that.

246
00:15:39,122 --> 00:15:44,282
No, but I doubt she approves of
the working class learning to read.

247
00:15:45,922 --> 00:15:48,522
Tom, can I take this chance to say

248
00:15:48,522 --> 00:15:52,602
you've managed a very delicate
transition superbly?

249
00:15:52,602 --> 00:15:54,602
Thank you.

250
00:15:55,642 --> 00:15:58,002
But don't be too eager to please.

251
00:15:58,002 --> 00:16:00,042
You have a new identity.

252
00:16:00,042 --> 00:16:03,842
And I don't mean because
you're not a chauffeur any more.

253
00:16:03,842 --> 00:16:06,962
You are the agent of this estate,

254
00:16:06,962 --> 00:16:09,242
and as the agent,
you have a perfect right

255
00:16:09,242 --> 00:16:11,762
to talk to anyone
who works under you.

256
00:16:13,402 --> 00:16:15,402
Anyone you choose.

257
00:16:16,762 --> 00:16:18,762
That's quite a speech.
I mean it.

258
00:16:19,762 --> 00:16:23,562
You have a position now,
and you're entitled to use it.

259
00:16:30,242 --> 00:16:32,282
Mr Branson!

260
00:16:32,282 --> 00:16:35,642
I didn't want to drag one of you
upstairs to open the door.

261
00:16:35,642 --> 00:16:38,122
That was kind.

262
00:16:38,122 --> 00:16:41,562
I wondered if you would allow
the maids to clean during the day

263
00:16:41,562 --> 00:16:43,722
while the family's in Scotland?

264
00:16:43,722 --> 00:16:45,882
You don't need my permission.

265
00:16:45,882 --> 00:16:47,962
But I do.

266
00:16:47,962 --> 00:16:51,442
And if you want to use a particular
room, then please let me know

267
00:16:51,442 --> 00:16:53,042
and we'll vacate it at once.

268
00:16:54,962 --> 00:16:56,642
Thank you, Mrs Hughes.

269
00:16:56,642 --> 00:16:59,482
Good night.
Good night, Mr Branson.

270
00:17:02,282 --> 00:17:04,282
I was a bit shaken up on the train.

271
00:17:05,322 --> 00:17:08,322
Please don't say anything -
I don't want to worry Mr Crawley.

272
00:17:08,322 --> 00:17:11,362
You don't want to give him
the satisfaction, you mean.

273
00:17:12,402 --> 00:17:16,402
I can't spoil his last treat
before fatherhood claims him.

274
00:17:18,082 --> 00:17:21,162
Not that he'll change his ways
much, if he's like most men.

275
00:17:23,762 --> 00:17:25,802
Are they looking after you?

276
00:17:25,802 --> 00:17:27,402
Oh, yes.

277
00:17:29,042 --> 00:17:32,682
But I'm a bit nervous
about this Ghillies' Ball.

278
00:17:32,682 --> 00:17:34,682
Why?

279
00:17:35,762 --> 00:17:37,642
I suppose I just feel so...

280
00:17:38,682 --> 00:17:40,722
..English.

281
00:17:40,722 --> 00:17:42,722
I don't want to look a fool.

282
00:17:43,802 --> 00:17:47,562
I love reeling. If I weren't
pregnant, I'd dance until dawn.

283
00:17:49,082 --> 00:17:51,082
But you ARE pregnant, milady.

284
00:17:58,602 --> 00:18:00,882
(BAGPIPER STRIKES UP)

285
00:18:11,522 --> 00:18:13,522
Bloody hell!

286
00:18:17,282 --> 00:18:19,282
Welcome to the Highlands.

287
00:18:25,402 --> 00:18:27,682
Now take your time.

288
00:18:27,682 --> 00:18:31,722
You're not chasing a pheasant.
Be calm and confident.

289
00:18:31,722 --> 00:18:34,242
I thought I was.

290
00:18:34,242 --> 00:18:35,842
These are noble beasts.

291
00:18:35,842 --> 00:18:38,322
We must take them out
for the good of the herd,

292
00:18:38,322 --> 00:18:41,602
but they've earned our respect
and deserve a clean death.

293
00:18:41,602 --> 00:18:43,682
Fine words.

294
00:18:43,682 --> 00:18:45,882
How are you getting on?

295
00:18:45,882 --> 00:18:48,082
Not too bad, Your Lordship.

296
00:18:48,082 --> 00:18:51,122
That's high praise from Nield.
He's being kind.

297
00:18:51,122 --> 00:18:53,482
I doubt your father
needs much practice.

298
00:18:53,482 --> 00:18:57,882
His Lordship was born with a rod
in one hand and a gun in the other.

299
00:18:57,882 --> 00:18:59,762
That sounds rather uncomfortable.

300
00:18:59,762 --> 00:19:01,562
I love to hear your ghillie speak.

301
00:19:01,562 --> 00:19:03,322
It's like a voice from
a bygone age.

302
00:19:03,322 --> 00:19:06,482
Where's Susan? Aunt Violet
wanted to see the gardens,

303
00:19:06,482 --> 00:19:08,482
so I left them to it.

304
00:19:10,042 --> 00:19:13,602
Rose is not anxious
for her mother's company.

305
00:19:13,602 --> 00:19:15,962
How's it going with you?

306
00:19:15,962 --> 00:19:17,922
I'm in for an adventure.

307
00:19:17,922 --> 00:19:21,682
I'm to don a ceremonial uniform
and hold mighty sway

308
00:19:21,682 --> 00:19:23,562
on some distant shore.

309
00:19:23,562 --> 00:19:25,322
You won't mind a foreign posting?

310
00:19:25,322 --> 00:19:27,442
Why not?

311
00:19:27,442 --> 00:19:30,962
They say a change of sink
is as good as a rest.

312
00:19:30,962 --> 00:19:33,322
Hello!

313
00:19:33,322 --> 00:19:35,322
You do know where it will be?

314
00:19:35,322 --> 00:19:39,602
No, but it will be filthy and
dirty, and the food will be awful

315
00:19:39,602 --> 00:19:42,722
and there will be no-one to talk to
for a hundred square miles.

316
00:19:42,722 --> 00:19:45,562
It sounds like a week
with my mother-in-law.

317
00:19:47,562 --> 00:19:49,602
Will Rose go too?
Why?

318
00:19:49,602 --> 00:19:51,602
What's she been saying?

319
00:19:52,602 --> 00:19:55,562
My dear, no-one can accuse me
of being modern,

320
00:19:55,562 --> 00:19:58,682
but even I can see
it's no crime to be young.

321
00:19:58,682 --> 00:20:01,122
I know that, Aunt Violet.

322
00:20:01,122 --> 00:20:03,962
But you don't see
how they gang up on me.

323
00:20:03,962 --> 00:20:07,322
Then I shall strive
to keep the peace.

324
00:20:07,322 --> 00:20:11,322
That's all very well,
but you are my mother's sister,

325
00:20:11,322 --> 00:20:13,362
and you can jolly well
be on my side.

326
00:20:19,602 --> 00:20:21,602
I'd like to travel more.

327
00:20:21,602 --> 00:20:26,242
We see a bit of London in the
season, otherwise it's Yorkshire.

328
00:20:26,242 --> 00:20:28,842
What about the House of Lords?

329
00:20:28,842 --> 00:20:31,962
When His Lordship goes up,
he just takes Mr Bates with him

330
00:20:31,962 --> 00:20:34,562
and stays in his club,
which is no use to me.

331
00:20:35,882 --> 00:20:38,282
We're headed
for an outpost of empire.

332
00:20:39,242 --> 00:20:42,042
Her Ladyship's dreading it,
and so am I.

333
00:20:42,042 --> 00:20:44,802
Oh, I don't know.

334
00:20:44,802 --> 00:20:49,042
Something different -
I could quite fancy that.

335
00:20:49,042 --> 00:20:51,082
Well, not me.

336
00:20:51,082 --> 00:20:53,442
All sweat and gyppy tummy? Oh, no.

337
00:21:01,522 --> 00:21:03,122
(CLEARS THROAT)

338
00:21:13,762 --> 00:21:15,802
Did you get him?
I did.

339
00:21:15,802 --> 00:21:17,842
He said he'd love to come.

340
00:21:17,842 --> 00:21:21,322
I'm sure he would.
What do you mean by that?

341
00:21:21,322 --> 00:21:24,962
I can't imagine Mr Gregson finds
himself at Duneagle Castle often.

342
00:21:24,962 --> 00:21:28,042
Or anywhere like it.
Mary, that sounds very snobbish.

343
00:21:28,042 --> 00:21:29,962
What's he doing up here?

344
00:21:29,962 --> 00:21:33,682
He's on a sketching holiday.
He's sketching and fishing.

345
00:21:33,682 --> 00:21:37,242
Fishing? Oh, well,
that's something, I suppose.

346
00:21:37,242 --> 00:21:39,242
What do you suppose?

347
00:21:39,242 --> 00:21:43,682
For some reason, Mary's decided
to be nasty about Michael Gregson.

348
00:21:43,682 --> 00:21:46,722
I was questioning his motives
for being in the Highlands.

349
00:21:46,722 --> 00:21:49,682
He's brought his pencils and his
rods. What's wrong with that?

350
00:21:49,682 --> 00:21:52,522
Nothing at all, so there.

351
00:22:05,842 --> 00:22:08,322
Afternoon, George.

352
00:22:08,322 --> 00:22:10,402
Can you do me a sandwich, please?

353
00:22:15,522 --> 00:22:20,162
This is very daring -
sitting in a pub on your own.

354
00:22:21,202 --> 00:22:23,642
Want the village
to cut you off dead forever?

355
00:22:25,122 --> 00:22:27,162
I knew you were coming in,

356
00:22:27,162 --> 00:22:29,242
and I don't care
about all that stuff.

357
00:22:29,242 --> 00:22:31,242
Do you have the day off?

358
00:22:31,242 --> 00:22:35,202
I'll fudge it, but no. We've extra
cleaning while the family's away.

359
00:22:35,202 --> 00:22:38,242
Of course, YOUR family.

360
00:22:38,242 --> 00:22:40,762
Well, I am and I'm not,
as I'm sure you know.

361
00:22:44,522 --> 00:22:49,322
Anna said when you first came back
as Lady Sybil's husband,

362
00:22:49,322 --> 00:22:51,762
you refused to dress the part,
but you do now.

363
00:22:54,882 --> 00:22:57,042
I was tired
of talking about my clothes

364
00:22:57,042 --> 00:22:59,282
every time I came downstairs.

365
00:23:00,802 --> 00:23:02,802
I'm still the same man inside.

366
00:23:05,802 --> 00:23:09,802
Then why not join us for dinner
one night, instead of eating alone?

367
00:23:13,682 --> 00:23:15,762
Well, tell her
I'll not forget again.

368
00:23:15,762 --> 00:23:18,802
By heck, it were worth a visit,
Mr Tufton. What a range!

369
00:23:18,802 --> 00:23:21,082
I've never heard
of some of those spices.

370
00:23:21,082 --> 00:23:23,882
I'll tell you what. We've got
a fair starting here on Friday.

371
00:23:23,882 --> 00:23:26,522
I run a stall,
and so do some of the suppliers.

372
00:23:26,522 --> 00:23:29,162
Why not come?
What sort of fair?

373
00:23:29,162 --> 00:23:33,602
Well, the usual sort of fair.
Food, games, Morris dancers...

374
00:23:33,602 --> 00:23:36,762
Can we get the time off?
I don't see why not. Jimmy?

375
00:23:36,762 --> 00:23:40,082
Well, I might come if there's
a crowd of us, but not otherwise.

376
00:23:40,082 --> 00:23:41,682
Have you got a minute?

377
00:23:42,682 --> 00:23:45,122
I'll just put a note in that bag
for Mrs Patmore.

378
00:23:45,122 --> 00:23:46,722
Go on, then.

379
00:23:49,842 --> 00:23:53,682
I can't let them go gallivanting off
to every fair at the drop of a hat.

380
00:23:53,682 --> 00:23:55,722
I mean, what are we paying them for?

381
00:23:55,722 --> 00:23:57,402
They've been working very hard.

382
00:23:57,402 --> 00:23:59,282
Don't they deserve a treat?
Excuse me.

383
00:24:00,962 --> 00:24:03,602
On Friday,
can I take the afternoon off?

384
00:24:03,602 --> 00:24:06,762
I'll make the servants' dinner
and Ivy and Daisy can serve it.

385
00:24:06,762 --> 00:24:10,682
Mrs Patmore doesn't often
take the time she's allowed.

386
00:24:10,682 --> 00:24:14,242
What about Mr Branson?
I'll see to Mr Branson.

387
00:24:14,242 --> 00:24:16,522
Where are you going,
or shouldn't we ask?

388
00:24:16,522 --> 00:24:19,082
There's a fair in Thirsk.

389
00:24:19,082 --> 00:24:21,602
A friend of mine has asked me
to meet him there.

390
00:24:22,682 --> 00:24:24,602
I don't believe it.

391
00:24:24,602 --> 00:24:27,602
Must I be undermined at every turn?

392
00:24:27,602 --> 00:24:29,042
Ooh!

393
00:24:30,602 --> 00:24:32,642
What's got into him?

394
00:24:33,602 --> 00:24:36,682
Mr Barrow and the boys
have asked to go to the same fair,

395
00:24:36,682 --> 00:24:39,162
and he was trying to find a way
to say no.

396
00:24:39,162 --> 00:24:40,962
Why don't we all go?

397
00:24:40,962 --> 00:24:44,482
I'll make Mr Branson a tray and
he can keep charge of the house.

398
00:24:44,482 --> 00:24:47,842
He won't mind. Come on.

399
00:24:47,842 --> 00:24:49,762
It'll be fun.

400
00:24:49,762 --> 00:24:51,802
(MURMUR OF VOICES)

401
00:24:51,802 --> 00:24:53,402
Mr Gregson!

402
00:24:54,882 --> 00:24:58,122
This is very kind of you,
Lady Flintshire. Not a bit.

403
00:24:58,122 --> 00:25:00,962
It's a pleasure to welcome a friend
of dear Edith's.

404
00:25:00,962 --> 00:25:03,842
What a disappointment.
He looks perfectly normal.

405
00:25:03,842 --> 00:25:07,242
Since he came with the express
purpose of dining at Duneagle,

406
00:25:07,242 --> 00:25:08,882
he obviously bought a set of tails.

407
00:25:08,882 --> 00:25:10,962
Come and meet my parents.

408
00:25:10,962 --> 00:25:13,682
Mama, Granny, Papa,

409
00:25:13,682 --> 00:25:17,242
this is Mr Gregson.
Lady Grantham.

410
00:25:17,242 --> 00:25:20,962
You know, I started to read your
magazine because of Edith's column,

411
00:25:20,962 --> 00:25:22,522
but now I wouldn't miss it.

412
00:25:22,522 --> 00:25:26,162
It puzzles me why you choose to
employ amateurs like my daughter.

413
00:25:26,162 --> 00:25:29,962
I agree. Well, is the distinction
very meaningful?

414
00:25:29,962 --> 00:25:33,842
Surely the most important thing is
whether she has something to say?

415
00:25:35,042 --> 00:25:36,722
Come and meet my sister Mary.

416
00:25:40,042 --> 00:25:42,722
Do stand up.
You're slouching like a field hand.

417
00:25:42,722 --> 00:25:46,082
Might I just have five minutes
without being criticised?

418
00:25:46,082 --> 00:25:49,042
We knew things were awkward
between them.

419
00:25:50,082 --> 00:25:53,602
But now that I'm here, I don't think
Susan handles it very well.

420
00:25:53,602 --> 00:25:57,882
It is so complicated with a young
daughter who's full of new ideas.

421
00:25:57,882 --> 00:25:59,802
She thinks you're fighting her,

422
00:25:59,802 --> 00:26:02,242
when all the time,
you're just frightened.

423
00:26:02,242 --> 00:26:04,282
I'm sorry.

424
00:26:07,322 --> 00:26:09,322
We all miss her.

425
00:26:09,322 --> 00:26:11,842
Every single day.

426
00:26:16,322 --> 00:26:18,082
We're going in, everyone.

427
00:26:20,722 --> 00:26:22,722
(KNOCK AT DOOR)
Come in.

428
00:26:24,162 --> 00:26:26,162
Who's that?

429
00:26:26,162 --> 00:26:30,162
Would you like me to take Miss
Sibby up to the nursery? Thank you.

430
00:26:30,162 --> 00:26:32,162
Go with Edna, darling.

431
00:26:32,162 --> 00:26:34,202
Come here.

432
00:26:41,602 --> 00:26:43,522
Can I ask you something?

433
00:26:43,522 --> 00:26:45,562
Be my guest. Please.

434
00:26:47,962 --> 00:26:50,602
Are you ashamed of who you are,
or of who you were?

435
00:26:50,602 --> 00:26:53,442
Is that why you won't
eat your dinner with us?

436
00:26:58,522 --> 00:26:59,922
No.

437
00:27:00,962 --> 00:27:02,602
It is not.

438
00:27:02,602 --> 00:27:04,602
Well, I'd better be going.

439
00:27:11,800 --> 00:27:14,080
It never really gets dark here,
does it?

440
00:27:14,080 --> 00:27:16,720
Not like further south, no.

441
00:27:18,680 --> 00:27:21,560
Let's take a picnic out tomorrow.
Just the two of us.

442
00:27:21,560 --> 00:27:24,360
They'll be gone for the day.
What do you say?

443
00:27:24,360 --> 00:27:26,360
I'd love it.

444
00:27:28,400 --> 00:27:30,040
(SOBS)

445
00:27:32,880 --> 00:27:34,880
Is everything all right, milady?

446
00:27:38,080 --> 00:27:41,400
It will be, if you don't tell
my mother you saw me smoking.

447
00:27:41,400 --> 00:27:44,840
Don't worry - you're safe with us.
Would you like a peppermint?

448
00:27:48,360 --> 00:27:50,360
I better had. Thank you.

449
00:27:52,160 --> 00:27:54,200
Oh, sorry.

450
00:27:54,200 --> 00:27:58,120
It's just my mother has been
unusually impossible this evening.

451
00:27:58,120 --> 00:28:01,360
My whole childhood would seem
impossible to you, milady,

452
00:28:01,360 --> 00:28:03,800
but I survived - so will you.

453
00:28:05,240 --> 00:28:08,560
Rose, who are you talking to?

454
00:28:08,560 --> 00:28:10,720
Shh.
Come inside at once.

455
00:28:10,720 --> 00:28:12,920
Everyone's in the drawing-room.

456
00:28:14,840 --> 00:28:16,560
Thanks for the mint.

457
00:28:19,520 --> 00:28:21,160
I should be away.

458
00:28:21,160 --> 00:28:23,560
Oh, don't go just yet.

459
00:28:23,560 --> 00:28:26,000
Heaven knows I've no desire to.

460
00:28:27,080 --> 00:28:30,240
It makes a welcome change
from reading a medical journal

461
00:28:30,240 --> 00:28:33,640
and going to bed with,
well, a glass of whisky.

462
00:28:33,640 --> 00:28:37,480
Goodness - I wondered what you
were going to say for a moment!

463
00:28:38,600 --> 00:28:42,120
I sometimes forget, when we meet
in the splendour of the abbey,

464
00:28:42,120 --> 00:28:44,840
that you were a doctor's wife.

465
00:28:44,840 --> 00:28:48,600
That you know what my life consists
of in a way that no-one else does.

466
00:28:48,600 --> 00:28:50,560
At any rate, not around here.

467
00:28:50,560 --> 00:28:52,240
I know.

468
00:28:52,240 --> 00:28:56,160
It's a relief to be able to talk
without having to explain oneself,
isn't it?

469
00:28:56,160 --> 00:28:59,960
A relief, and a privilege.

470
00:28:59,960 --> 00:29:02,800
And I hope we can do it again.

471
00:29:02,800 --> 00:29:04,080
Soon.

472
00:29:06,720 --> 00:29:09,880
Matthew's asked me to go out
stalking with him tomorrow.

473
00:29:09,880 --> 00:29:11,920
So I thought I would.

474
00:29:11,920 --> 00:29:15,840
Michael, can I ask you
why you're here?

475
00:29:15,840 --> 00:29:19,120
Tell me the truth. Please.

476
00:29:19,120 --> 00:29:21,640
Well, I want to get
to know your family.

477
00:29:22,680 --> 00:29:24,680
What do you hope to achieve?

478
00:29:25,880 --> 00:29:31,760
I thought if they knew me,
if they came to like me,

479
00:29:31,760 --> 00:29:36,280
they might find it easier
to be on my side.

480
00:29:37,600 --> 00:29:40,560
It won't change the basic facts,
though, will it?

481
00:29:40,560 --> 00:29:43,720
Edith, my basic fact is
that I'm in love with you.

482
00:29:46,280 --> 00:29:50,120
You know that already.
Do I?

483
00:29:50,120 --> 00:29:52,320
Yes, I suppose I do.

484
00:29:52,320 --> 00:29:54,320
I want you in my life.

485
00:29:55,880 --> 00:29:57,800
And I want to be in yours.

486
00:29:58,960 --> 00:30:01,360
That's all very well, but...

487
00:30:03,360 --> 00:30:07,160
..I just can't see a happy ending.

488
00:30:07,160 --> 00:30:08,960
Edith, dear.

489
00:30:08,960 --> 00:30:12,920
Stop fascinating that young man and
come and make a four at bridge.

490
00:30:13,960 --> 00:30:15,800
(SIGHS)

491
00:30:17,640 --> 00:30:19,560
I've asked Gregson to come.

492
00:30:19,560 --> 00:30:22,000
I won't see you all day,
so he'll be company.

493
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:25,000
He was right to invest
in those tails, wasn't he?

494
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:28,040
You know Susan's invited him
to the Ghillies' Ball?

495
00:30:28,040 --> 00:30:32,120
He probably had reeling classes
before he left London.

496
00:30:32,120 --> 00:30:34,960
Don't dislike him
before you know him.

497
00:30:35,960 --> 00:30:39,320
That's the hallmark of our parents'
generation, and I forbid it.

498
00:30:42,280 --> 00:30:44,320
Just be as nice as you are.

499
00:30:44,320 --> 00:30:46,520
You think me nice,
but nobody else does.

500
00:30:47,760 --> 00:30:49,800
What makes you so sure I am?

501
00:30:49,800 --> 00:30:54,840
Because I've seen you naked,
and held you in my arms.

502
00:30:54,840 --> 00:30:57,400
And I know the real you.

503
00:30:58,400 --> 00:31:00,400
Goodness, what a testimonial.

504
00:31:06,280 --> 00:31:09,240
Oh, go on. You were young once.

505
00:31:09,240 --> 00:31:11,280
I'm young now.

506
00:31:11,280 --> 00:31:13,280
Well, not old.

507
00:31:14,400 --> 00:31:16,440
All the more reason to say yes.

508
00:31:16,440 --> 00:31:18,160
Oh, you'll enjoy yourself.

509
00:31:18,160 --> 00:31:20,800
No. I won't be coming.

510
00:31:24,120 --> 00:31:27,160
If I came, they wouldn't have fun.

511
00:31:27,160 --> 00:31:30,080
They'd spend the day
looking over their shoulder.

512
00:31:30,080 --> 00:31:33,680
Well, I'm going,
whether I spoil their fun or not.

513
00:31:33,680 --> 00:31:36,360
That's different.
They respect you, of course,

514
00:31:36,360 --> 00:31:38,240
but I am their leader.

515
00:31:39,480 --> 00:31:42,080
Well, that's put me in my place.

516
00:31:42,080 --> 00:31:45,040
Don't envy me, Mrs Hughes.

517
00:31:45,040 --> 00:31:47,080
You know what they say.

518
00:31:47,080 --> 00:31:50,880
'Uneasy lies the head
that wears the crown.'

519
00:31:53,120 --> 00:31:55,640
The wind is north-west
and getting stronger.

520
00:31:55,640 --> 00:31:57,080
We should get started.

521
00:31:57,080 --> 00:31:59,480
Well, good hunting, everyone.

522
00:31:59,480 --> 00:32:02,360
Oh, Shrimpie's not stalking.
He's coming with me.

523
00:32:02,360 --> 00:32:04,520
Mr Crawley? Mr Gregson?

524
00:32:05,760 --> 00:32:08,280
Is your picnic under control?
Of course.

525
00:32:08,280 --> 00:32:11,040
I'm worried about Mary
bumping through the glen.

526
00:32:11,040 --> 00:32:13,440
She doesn't have to come.
No, I know.

527
00:32:13,440 --> 00:32:17,600
But she will. We'd best get going,
or we're in for some stick.

528
00:32:25,800 --> 00:32:27,400
Walk on.

529
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:41,280
I'm not sure I'm going tomorrow.
It's such a waste of money.

530
00:32:41,280 --> 00:32:46,000
Oh, come on. You've enough for
a few rides and a beef sandwich.

531
00:32:46,000 --> 00:32:47,800
I can buy you all a bottle of pop.

532
00:32:47,800 --> 00:32:51,720
What an offer! Let's take him up
on it, before he thinks again.

533
00:32:51,720 --> 00:32:54,000
Thank you, Mr Barrow,
but I can buy my own pop.

534
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:56,080
Don't pretend you've money to burn.

535
00:32:56,080 --> 00:32:58,080
I can always get money.

536
00:32:58,080 --> 00:33:01,320
The store cupboard's open
if you need anything.

537
00:33:01,320 --> 00:33:03,520
No, but I do have something
to ask you.

538
00:33:03,520 --> 00:33:05,560
Now, where did I put that box?

539
00:33:05,560 --> 00:33:07,480
This one?

540
00:33:12,760 --> 00:33:16,040
Did you see the lid?
Mrs Kearney's dress shop in Ripon.

541
00:33:16,040 --> 00:33:18,840
She's got a fancy man,
I'm telling you.

542
00:33:18,840 --> 00:33:22,200
Mrs Patmore?
Why not? She's a woman, isn't she?

543
00:33:22,200 --> 00:33:24,040
Only technically.

544
00:33:25,080 --> 00:33:27,080
(BIRDSONG)

545
00:33:29,720 --> 00:33:34,080
Oh. I was looking for Lady Mary
to tell her we're going.

546
00:33:34,080 --> 00:33:36,080
She's already gone down, milady.

547
00:33:39,720 --> 00:33:41,880
You were kind to cheer me up,
yesterday.

548
00:33:41,880 --> 00:33:44,200
I did feel terribly blue.

549
00:33:44,200 --> 00:33:45,840
That's all right.

550
00:33:45,840 --> 00:33:48,680
You must let me know
if I can return the favour.

551
00:33:49,720 --> 00:33:51,320
As a matter of fact...

552
00:33:52,720 --> 00:33:55,280
..there is something
you could help me with.

553
00:34:40,880 --> 00:34:43,360
(Shall I take it from here?)

554
00:34:50,160 --> 00:34:52,160
We can bring it to a better place.

555
00:34:53,200 --> 00:34:55,240
We don't rush things at Duneagle.

556
00:34:55,240 --> 00:34:58,040
Well, that's true, God knows!

557
00:35:06,120 --> 00:35:08,320
You don't think it's too girlish?

558
00:35:08,320 --> 00:35:11,400
What's the matter with
being girlish, once in a while?

559
00:35:11,400 --> 00:35:14,440
Heaven knows
we don't have much opportunity.

560
00:35:14,440 --> 00:35:18,360
Well, I'd wear a coat over it,
so it wouldn't jump out at you.

561
00:35:18,360 --> 00:35:20,200
I hope he's worth it.

562
00:35:21,240 --> 00:35:23,240
Read that.

563
00:35:26,080 --> 00:35:30,960
'I hope you will allow me
the honour of squiring you
through the day.'

564
00:35:30,960 --> 00:35:35,200
No man's wanted to 'squire' me
since the Golden Jubilee.

565
00:35:35,200 --> 00:35:39,080
Even then, he expected me
to buy the drinks.

566
00:35:43,120 --> 00:35:45,600
Suppose he wants something more.

567
00:35:45,600 --> 00:35:47,760
I beg your pardon?

568
00:35:47,760 --> 00:35:53,280
There is only one reason a man
his age courts a respectable woman.

569
00:35:55,480 --> 00:35:58,000
He finds himself in need of a wife.

570
00:36:01,440 --> 00:36:03,440
(FLOWING WATER)

571
00:36:07,120 --> 00:36:09,160
Is there anything to drink?

572
00:36:09,160 --> 00:36:11,160
There certainly is.

573
00:36:15,640 --> 00:36:17,320
Beer!

574
00:36:18,560 --> 00:36:20,520
That's very racy of you.

575
00:36:20,520 --> 00:36:22,320
I am racy.

576
00:36:23,560 --> 00:36:25,560
What shall we drink to?

577
00:36:26,600 --> 00:36:28,640
The future.

578
00:36:28,640 --> 00:36:30,920
And your Scottish blood.

579
00:36:32,080 --> 00:36:33,960
What are you up to?

580
00:36:33,960 --> 00:36:35,200
Nothing.

581
00:36:37,040 --> 00:36:38,840
What are you up to?

582
00:36:38,840 --> 00:36:40,280
Nothing!

583
00:36:40,280 --> 00:36:42,400
(GIGGLES)

584
00:36:49,520 --> 00:36:51,800
Well, you managed that very well.

585
00:36:51,800 --> 00:36:55,840
When the time comes for me to go,
I'll ask them to send for you.

586
00:36:56,880 --> 00:36:58,880
Is everything all right, Shrimpie?

587
00:37:00,080 --> 00:37:02,480
Of course. Impertinent to ask.

588
00:37:04,840 --> 00:37:06,880
No, it's not all right.

589
00:37:06,880 --> 00:37:09,160
But what's the point
in talking about it

590
00:37:09,160 --> 00:37:11,200
when there's nothing to be done?

591
00:37:11,200 --> 00:37:14,160
I'm not so sure.
The Marlboroughs have got a divorce

592
00:37:14,160 --> 00:37:16,360
and you still see them around.

593
00:37:16,360 --> 00:37:18,840
But Sunny Marlborough
has no official post.

594
00:37:18,840 --> 00:37:21,240
He hasn't been in office
since the war.

595
00:37:21,240 --> 00:37:24,880
While you must have Susan
next to you under the tropical sun.

596
00:37:25,920 --> 00:37:27,520
She'll do it well.

597
00:37:29,480 --> 00:37:31,880
She can teach diplomacy to experts.

598
00:37:31,880 --> 00:37:33,880
But?

599
00:37:37,680 --> 00:37:39,680
We don't like each other.

600
00:37:51,000 --> 00:37:53,440
How can I help, Mr Branson?

601
00:37:57,160 --> 00:37:59,560
I was thinking...

602
00:38:01,560 --> 00:38:03,280
It's just...

603
00:38:03,280 --> 00:38:04,920
Yes?

604
00:38:04,920 --> 00:38:07,400
I thought I'd come down
for supper tonight.

605
00:38:07,400 --> 00:38:09,760
Catch up with your news.

606
00:38:09,760 --> 00:38:12,720
If you would like to, of course
you'd be very welcome.

607
00:38:12,720 --> 00:38:15,080
We don't eat late
while the family's away,

608
00:38:15,080 --> 00:38:17,480
so dinner will be
at about eight o'clock.

609
00:38:18,960 --> 00:38:21,240
I'll see you then.

610
00:38:23,360 --> 00:38:25,760
Fains I tell Mr Carson.

611
00:38:36,120 --> 00:38:38,160
How tiny the glens make one feel.

612
00:38:38,160 --> 00:38:41,240
That is the thing about nature.

613
00:38:41,240 --> 00:38:42,800
There's so much of it.

614
00:38:42,800 --> 00:38:45,720
Must be lovely to be queen
of such a kingdom.

615
00:38:45,720 --> 00:38:48,800
You're right.
We're very lucky in this.

616
00:38:49,840 --> 00:38:53,720
Goodness - we weren't expecting
male company for our feasting.

617
00:38:53,720 --> 00:38:55,840
I'm sorry to disappoint you.

618
00:38:57,640 --> 00:38:59,200
How did you get on?

619
00:38:59,200 --> 00:39:01,840
Very well. Not too long a trail,

620
00:39:01,840 --> 00:39:03,840
and death by a single shot
at the end of it.

621
00:39:03,840 --> 00:39:07,080
Nield is cock-a-hoop.
Well done, Papa.

622
00:39:07,080 --> 00:39:09,840
Your reward will be
to join the ladies' lunch.

623
00:39:09,840 --> 00:39:12,080
An added bonus.
I hope it's venison.

624
00:39:12,080 --> 00:39:14,160
Quite right.
We ought to eat what we kill.

625
00:39:14,160 --> 00:39:17,680
Stop talking nonsense and tell
McCree to lay two more places.

626
00:39:23,900 --> 00:39:26,540
10 hours crawling through heather

627
00:39:26,540 --> 00:39:28,420
and nothing to show for it.

628
00:39:29,700 --> 00:39:31,700
Perhaps it's a parable of life.

629
00:39:32,860 --> 00:39:34,900
Reminds me of the trenches rather.

630
00:39:34,900 --> 00:39:38,860
Hours of inching through mud
with no discernible purpose.

631
00:39:40,500 --> 00:39:42,220
(RUMBLE OF THUNDER)

632
00:39:43,500 --> 00:39:45,860
Why don't you come fly-fishing
tomorrow?

633
00:39:45,860 --> 00:39:48,380
We might see a bit more activity.

634
00:39:49,380 --> 00:39:52,740
You could bring your evening clothes
to change at Duneagle.

635
00:39:52,740 --> 00:39:54,780
That's rather an imposition.

636
00:39:54,780 --> 00:39:56,940
That's what you're here for,
isn't it?

637
00:39:56,940 --> 00:39:58,980
To get to know us all.

638
00:39:58,980 --> 00:40:02,700
And you didn't bring your tails all
this way to dine in a country pub.

639
00:40:04,300 --> 00:40:05,980
No.

640
00:40:05,980 --> 00:40:08,020
No, I suppose not.

641
00:40:08,020 --> 00:40:10,020
(PEAL OF THUNDER)

642
00:40:19,620 --> 00:40:21,460
Have you got a minute?

643
00:40:21,460 --> 00:40:24,500
Because, if you haven't,
it's perfectly fine.

644
00:40:24,500 --> 00:40:27,300
A minute for what?

645
00:40:27,300 --> 00:40:29,500
Her Ladyship would like a word.

646
00:40:29,500 --> 00:40:31,420
What does she want with me?

647
00:40:31,420 --> 00:40:33,700
Well, what do you think?

648
00:40:33,700 --> 00:40:36,340
She wants to make a fuss,
like she always does.

649
00:40:36,340 --> 00:40:39,140
(RUMBLE OF THUNDER)

650
00:40:39,140 --> 00:40:42,300
This is so kind of you...

651
00:40:42,300 --> 00:40:44,340
O'Brien, milady.

652
00:40:44,340 --> 00:40:49,140
..O'Brien. It's just that Wilkins
here isn't quite able to understand

653
00:40:49,140 --> 00:40:52,620
what I'm getting at when I'm
describing Lady Grantham's hair.

654
00:40:52,620 --> 00:40:55,580
I understand, milady, it's just -
Could you help her?

655
00:40:55,580 --> 00:40:57,620
You'll know exactly what I mean.

656
00:40:57,620 --> 00:40:59,500
Oh, er...

657
00:40:59,500 --> 00:41:02,300
Well, it's a question of body,
milady.

658
00:41:02,300 --> 00:41:06,620
You need more volume to begin,
before you sculpt it, so to speak.

659
00:41:06,620 --> 00:41:08,660
I knew you'd have the answer.

660
00:41:08,660 --> 00:41:11,940
If I could...
Oh, please, please, please.

661
00:41:15,420 --> 00:41:18,260
Wilkins, pay close attention
to what she's doing.

662
00:41:18,260 --> 00:41:20,940
Yes, Your Ladyship.

663
00:41:23,100 --> 00:41:26,740
Bombay? That sounds rather modest
for a marquis.

664
00:41:26,740 --> 00:41:30,060
Well, no, not if it's a step
towards the Viceroy's crown.

665
00:41:30,060 --> 00:41:33,820
I don't know about that. All I do
know is it's going to be very hot.

666
00:41:33,820 --> 00:41:36,700
And all the costumes
of imperial rule

667
00:41:36,700 --> 00:41:40,540
are always so peculiarly unsuited
to the climate.

668
00:41:40,540 --> 00:41:43,460
Will you take Rose?
I don't think we should,

669
00:41:43,460 --> 00:41:46,500
but Susan won't discuss it.

670
00:41:46,500 --> 00:41:50,660
Unless you want her married
to a third-rate colonial official

671
00:41:50,660 --> 00:41:53,900
with no money and bad teeth,
Susan had better think again.

672
00:41:53,900 --> 00:41:57,460
Just as he stood,
a gust of wind comes into the corrie
from behind us,

673
00:41:57,460 --> 00:41:59,700
a hind got our scent,
and they were away.

674
00:41:59,700 --> 00:42:04,020
Really, darling. It's boring enough
to hear about when you succeed...

675
00:42:04,020 --> 00:42:06,220
What did you think of Michael?

676
00:42:06,220 --> 00:42:09,940
He seems like a nice chap.
We're going fishing tomorrow.

677
00:42:09,940 --> 00:42:12,460
He's had such a lot to put up with.

678
00:42:12,460 --> 00:42:15,940
Oh, God. Not one of your
hard-luck cases, is he?

679
00:42:16,980 --> 00:42:19,020
Why must you sound so heartless?

680
00:42:19,020 --> 00:42:22,580
Actually, I think India
would be fascinating,

681
00:42:22,580 --> 00:42:25,980
but I know that Mummy and I
would drive each other mad.

682
00:42:25,980 --> 00:42:28,140
You mustn't be too hard
on your mother.

683
00:42:28,140 --> 00:42:31,140
You know, it's natural for her
to be concerned.

684
00:42:31,140 --> 00:42:33,180
Concerned?

685
00:42:33,180 --> 00:42:34,980
Is that what she is?

686
00:42:34,980 --> 00:42:37,820
I'm sure she loves you very much.

687
00:42:37,820 --> 00:42:39,980
What's this?

688
00:42:41,140 --> 00:42:44,460
Rose was just saying
how nice your hair looks tonight.

689
00:42:47,380 --> 00:42:49,220
He's what?

690
00:42:49,220 --> 00:42:51,860
It's only meant to be friendly.

691
00:42:51,860 --> 00:42:53,820
(KNOCK AT DOOR)

692
00:42:54,820 --> 00:42:56,820
Good evening, Mr Carson.

693
00:42:56,820 --> 00:42:59,380
I don't expect you to approve.

694
00:42:59,380 --> 00:43:01,580
Well, now. Come along in.

695
00:43:05,940 --> 00:43:09,980
I hope you won't show an example
of rudeness to the younger staff.

696
00:43:13,580 --> 00:43:15,620
I was hoping to catch you.

697
00:43:15,620 --> 00:43:17,660
What about a glass of something?

698
00:43:17,660 --> 00:43:21,100
No, I won't stay,
but I've had an idea.

699
00:43:21,100 --> 00:43:23,180
I saw Mrs Hughes
in the village today,

700
00:43:23,180 --> 00:43:26,860
and she told me the servants have
got up a party for the Thirsk fair.

701
00:43:26,860 --> 00:43:31,060
And, erm, I was wondering
if you'd like to go.

702
00:43:32,100 --> 00:43:35,500
What, with the servants?
No, of course not.

703
00:43:35,500 --> 00:43:38,540
But I could drive us over
for an hour or two.

704
00:43:38,540 --> 00:43:40,580
I'm told they do it well.

705
00:43:40,580 --> 00:43:42,220
Why not?

706
00:43:42,220 --> 00:43:44,100
It might be fun.

707
00:43:44,100 --> 00:43:46,900
Good. I'll come for you at five.

708
00:43:48,700 --> 00:43:52,140
Know what you're going to wear?
Nothing good, that's for sure.

709
00:43:53,300 --> 00:43:54,940
I'll keep an eye on the place.

710
00:43:54,940 --> 00:43:56,940
Oh, don't say you're not coming.

711
00:44:00,500 --> 00:44:02,220
I-I thought you could drive us.

712
00:44:02,220 --> 00:44:05,500
There is no need for impertinence,
Edna, thank you.

713
00:44:05,500 --> 00:44:07,860
You're all right.
I'm happy to drive them.

714
00:44:07,860 --> 00:44:11,220
But who'll stay here?
I will.

715
00:44:12,380 --> 00:44:15,100
You don't want to come to the fair?

716
00:44:15,100 --> 00:44:17,020
I would sooner chew broken glass.

717
00:44:24,140 --> 00:44:26,220
It was lovely, milady.

718
00:44:26,220 --> 00:44:29,780
But what about you?
Did you enjoy your day?

719
00:44:29,780 --> 00:44:31,820
Oh, I was stupid
to go to the picnic.

720
00:44:31,820 --> 00:44:34,660
We were shaken about in that trap
like dice in a cup.

721
00:44:34,660 --> 00:44:37,340
Stay in bed for the morning.

722
00:44:37,340 --> 00:44:39,860
And take it easy at the ball.

723
00:44:39,860 --> 00:44:42,780
Are you looking forward to it?

724
00:44:42,780 --> 00:44:47,260
I am rather. I've been planning
a bit of a surprise for Mr Bates.

725
00:44:47,260 --> 00:44:49,620
Why? What sort of surprise?

726
00:44:49,620 --> 00:44:52,500
No. It's a surprise for you, too.

727
00:44:55,020 --> 00:44:56,740
Don't forget what I said.

728
00:45:01,580 --> 00:45:03,860
What was that?

729
00:45:03,860 --> 00:45:07,060
Just that I've promised
to rest tomorrow.

730
00:45:07,060 --> 00:45:10,100
Which is annoying, because
I'd rather come out with you

731
00:45:10,100 --> 00:45:11,700
and interrogate Mr Gregson.

732
00:45:12,740 --> 00:45:14,380
Is he going to propose?

733
00:45:14,380 --> 00:45:18,140
I think so. But he's quite opaque.

734
00:45:18,140 --> 00:45:20,180
A man of mystery.

735
00:45:20,180 --> 00:45:23,300
Edith could use some of that.

736
00:45:23,300 --> 00:45:25,180
You are horrid when you want to be.

737
00:45:25,180 --> 00:45:28,220
I know, but you love me, don't you?

738
00:45:28,220 --> 00:45:30,220
Madly.

739
00:45:38,460 --> 00:45:40,580
(HUMS)

740
00:45:40,580 --> 00:45:42,620
And the man on your right.

741
00:45:42,620 --> 00:45:44,180
And...

742
00:45:46,260 --> 00:45:48,300
And then round,
in a figure of eight.

743
00:45:48,300 --> 00:45:50,300
(HUMS)

744
00:45:53,940 --> 00:45:55,940
(BOTH LAUGH)

745
00:45:59,900 --> 00:46:01,980
You take it easy
with Mr Barrow today.

746
00:46:01,980 --> 00:46:05,820
I don't mean...crawl all over him,

747
00:46:05,820 --> 00:46:07,860
but don't spoil things.

748
00:46:07,860 --> 00:46:09,940
You're a fine one to talk.

749
00:46:09,940 --> 00:46:11,820
Who rang the police
in the first place?

750
00:46:16,020 --> 00:46:18,020
Sod this. I'm bushed.

751
00:46:24,860 --> 00:46:26,740
Suppose someone comes in?

752
00:46:26,740 --> 00:46:29,100
They'll find a man
sitting in an armchair.

753
00:46:29,100 --> 00:46:30,860
They'll survive it.

754
00:46:37,900 --> 00:46:39,340
(SNIGGERS)

755
00:46:40,380 --> 00:46:43,980
Funny thing with Mr Barrow is,
he won't hear a bad word about you.

756
00:46:43,980 --> 00:46:46,300
Why? What have I done?

757
00:46:46,300 --> 00:46:49,020
I only meant he won't let anyone
speak against you.

758
00:46:52,060 --> 00:46:55,140
What on earth is going on in here?

759
00:46:55,140 --> 00:46:58,820
We were just - You were just taking
advantage of the cat's absence.

760
00:46:58,820 --> 00:47:00,860
We'll see what
Mr Carson has to say.

761
00:47:00,860 --> 00:47:03,020
Mrs Hughes.

762
00:47:03,020 --> 00:47:05,020
Mr Branson.

763
00:47:10,140 --> 00:47:12,780
What time are we leaving?
About half past four.

764
00:47:12,780 --> 00:47:15,420
But Mr Stark can easily drive us.

765
00:47:16,460 --> 00:47:18,100
Because I'm so high and mighty?

766
00:47:19,420 --> 00:47:21,540
You're part of the family now.

767
00:47:21,540 --> 00:47:24,060
There's nothing false in that.

768
00:47:24,060 --> 00:47:25,740
I know.

769
00:47:26,740 --> 00:47:28,780
I hope you do.

770
00:47:28,780 --> 00:47:33,020
Because if someone is trying
to make you feel awkward,

771
00:47:33,020 --> 00:47:35,340
they're in the wrong, not you.

772
00:47:37,380 --> 00:47:39,380
I'll be there at half past four.

773
00:47:47,699 --> 00:47:49,699
(WALTZ PLAYS)

774
00:47:56,099 --> 00:47:58,539
I said I'd meet him at his stall.

775
00:47:58,539 --> 00:48:01,619
What's that?
He asked me to bring sandwiches.

776
00:48:01,619 --> 00:48:04,899
Can I come? I want to find out
where the best food stalls are.

777
00:48:04,899 --> 00:48:07,459
Why don't we all go?
Here's something for us.

778
00:48:07,459 --> 00:48:10,459
Alfred? Mr Branson?
Let's give it a go.

779
00:48:11,459 --> 00:48:13,059
I don't mind.

780
00:48:14,219 --> 00:48:16,219
What about you?

781
00:48:17,259 --> 00:48:19,299
Isn't it a bit rough for Mr Barrow?

782
00:48:19,299 --> 00:48:21,299
Oh, I think I could manage.

783
00:48:22,859 --> 00:48:25,459
I'll come and cheer you on,
if that's all right.

784
00:48:26,499 --> 00:48:28,539
Wait till Mrs Hughes sees that!

785
00:48:28,539 --> 00:48:31,219
Come on.
Don't care about any tug-of-war.

786
00:48:31,219 --> 00:48:34,499
Let's go and find some games.
You do know they're all fixed?

787
00:48:34,499 --> 00:48:37,499
I don't think they are.
They must have seen you coming.

788
00:48:39,219 --> 00:48:41,259
Come on, ladies -
drink up, drink up.

789
00:48:41,259 --> 00:48:43,379
We can go and join in the fun then.

790
00:48:43,379 --> 00:48:45,139
What about your stall?

791
00:48:45,139 --> 00:48:47,419
Lucy can look after the stall,
can't you, Lucy?

792
00:48:48,619 --> 00:48:49,899
Cheeky!

793
00:48:49,899 --> 00:48:53,339
I hope you don't mind my saying so,
Mrs Patmore,

794
00:48:53,339 --> 00:48:56,939
but in that blouse, you look as if
you've just stepped out of Vogue.

795
00:48:57,979 --> 00:48:59,779
I don't mind.

796
00:48:59,779 --> 00:49:03,259
I don't believe you, but I don't
mind you saying it at all.

797
00:49:03,259 --> 00:49:05,659
You're generous with compliments.

798
00:49:05,659 --> 00:49:08,339
I love to be in love, Mrs Hughes,
I'll not deny it.

799
00:49:08,339 --> 00:49:11,699
Any time, any place -
I love to be in love!

800
00:49:11,699 --> 00:49:13,459
Get away, you daft beggar!

801
00:49:14,739 --> 00:49:18,299
I must find Alfred and tell him
where to look for the spice stall.

802
00:49:18,299 --> 00:49:20,779
If you don't mind my leaving you?

803
00:49:20,779 --> 00:49:23,019
No, I don't mind that, either.

804
00:49:46,819 --> 00:49:48,619
Oh, yes!

805
00:49:48,619 --> 00:49:50,619
(CHEERING)

806
00:49:51,979 --> 00:49:53,819
Any side bets before we begin?

807
00:49:53,819 --> 00:49:55,739
Who'd bet on them?

808
00:49:55,739 --> 00:49:58,139
What odds would you give us?

809
00:49:58,139 --> 00:50:00,219
10 to one.

810
00:50:00,219 --> 00:50:03,299
Right. A quid on the Downton team.

811
00:50:03,299 --> 00:50:04,819
(LAUGHS)

812
00:50:06,739 --> 00:50:10,299
Any more?
That's enough money down the drain.

813
00:50:10,299 --> 00:50:11,539
Ready?

814
00:50:12,619 --> 00:50:14,899
One moment.

815
00:50:14,899 --> 00:50:18,459
Mr Tufton,
you'll join our team, won't you?

816
00:50:18,459 --> 00:50:20,259
As a Downton supplier?

817
00:50:20,259 --> 00:50:22,059
If you want us, lad, aye.

818
00:50:24,139 --> 00:50:26,979
Had you already seen him?
What do you think?

819
00:50:26,979 --> 00:50:28,979
Will you be so kind?

820
00:50:31,499 --> 00:50:33,739
Tufton's at your service.

821
00:50:33,739 --> 00:50:35,899
Good afternoon, ladies.

822
00:50:35,899 --> 00:50:39,019
They needed a bit of muscle,
so they sent for Tufton. Feel that.

823
00:50:39,019 --> 00:50:41,059
Eh?
Very strong.

824
00:50:41,059 --> 00:50:43,059
That's my name - Joss Tufton.

825
00:50:43,059 --> 00:50:45,739
See that stall with the spices on?
That's me.

826
00:50:45,739 --> 00:50:48,339
If you want a bit of spice in
your life, send for Tufton.

827
00:50:48,339 --> 00:50:50,259
Take the strain!

828
00:50:52,059 --> 00:50:54,139
Pull!

829
00:50:54,139 --> 00:50:56,139
(SHOUTS OF ENCOURAGEMENT)

830
00:50:58,419 --> 00:51:01,059
Of course it's a lot to ask,
but what else can I do?

831
00:51:02,059 --> 00:51:04,499
I'm prevented
from divorcing a woman who...

832
00:51:05,499 --> 00:51:07,539
..doesn't even know who I am.

833
00:51:07,539 --> 00:51:10,499
Does the law expect me
to have no life at all until I die?

834
00:51:10,499 --> 00:51:12,099
Would Lord Grantham?

835
00:51:13,339 --> 00:51:15,819
My father-in-law
would be the first to understand

836
00:51:15,819 --> 00:51:19,299
you have to make some sort of
life for yourself beyond the pale.

837
00:51:19,299 --> 00:51:23,779
I do. You can't expect him to want
you to involve his own daughter.

838
00:51:23,779 --> 00:51:27,299
Not when all you have to offer
is a job as your mistress.

839
00:51:27,299 --> 00:51:28,939
No. I love her.

840
00:51:30,139 --> 00:51:32,179
I...

841
00:51:32,179 --> 00:51:33,779
I'm offering my love.

842
00:51:34,779 --> 00:51:37,899
You've been misled
by our surroundings.

843
00:51:39,339 --> 00:51:42,059
We're not in a novel by
Walter Scott.

844
00:51:45,819 --> 00:51:47,819
(CHEERING)

845
00:51:49,219 --> 00:51:52,419
Come on, lads! Come on, boys!

846
00:51:57,259 --> 00:51:59,219
Come on, Mr Branson!

847
00:52:02,379 --> 00:52:04,259
Mr Tufton!

848
00:52:05,699 --> 00:52:07,659
Pull it! Pull the rope!

849
00:52:07,659 --> 00:52:09,659
Come on, boys!

850
00:52:14,579 --> 00:52:15,939
(CHEERING)

851
00:52:15,939 --> 00:52:20,059
I declare the Downton team
the winner!

852
00:52:22,179 --> 00:52:24,699
Thank you very much.

853
00:52:33,819 --> 00:52:36,499
Well done, Jimmy.
Thank you, Mr Branson.

854
00:52:38,419 --> 00:52:43,699
So the laws of society should
be preserved, no matter what?

855
00:52:43,699 --> 00:52:47,139
Edith gave me the impression
you were a freer soul than that.

856
00:52:47,139 --> 00:52:49,139
I find that hard to believe.

857
00:52:52,019 --> 00:52:56,219
I agree your position is tragic
and I'm very sorry.

858
00:52:57,859 --> 00:53:01,659
You can't imagine I would let Edith
slide into a life of scandal

859
00:53:01,659 --> 00:53:03,739
without lifting a finger
to stop her?

860
00:53:03,739 --> 00:53:05,779
Will you tell Lord Grantham?

861
00:53:05,779 --> 00:53:10,419
I'm not going to tell anyone, but
you must see it's quite hopeless.

862
00:53:10,419 --> 00:53:12,459
You're saying I should leave now?

863
00:53:12,459 --> 00:53:15,739
Not stay for the ball?
No.

864
00:53:15,739 --> 00:53:18,299
Use it to say a proper goodbye.

865
00:53:19,339 --> 00:53:21,059
You owe her that.

866
00:53:31,579 --> 00:53:33,219
Oh, don't!

867
00:53:33,219 --> 00:53:35,259
Oh! Sorry!

868
00:53:35,259 --> 00:53:36,539
(GIGGLES)

869
00:53:41,779 --> 00:53:43,779
Let's have a drink.

870
00:53:45,059 --> 00:53:47,899
One all round, all right?

871
00:53:47,899 --> 00:53:50,939
My pleasure, lads, my pleasure.

872
00:53:50,939 --> 00:53:52,939
Hoop yourselves a fortune, ladies.

873
00:53:53,979 --> 00:53:55,459
Thruppence for three.

874
00:53:55,459 --> 00:53:57,939
Thruppence? Never in this world.

875
00:53:57,939 --> 00:53:59,579
Look at the prizes, eh?

876
00:53:59,579 --> 00:54:01,819
Not fairground rubbish here,
you know.

877
00:54:01,819 --> 00:54:03,739
When did you last see
a gold sovereign?

878
00:54:03,739 --> 00:54:05,219
When did you last win one?

879
00:54:05,219 --> 00:54:06,899
What's the matter?

880
00:54:06,899 --> 00:54:09,659
I want a go, but Daisy
thinks it's too expensive.

881
00:54:09,659 --> 00:54:11,659
Oh, have it on me.

882
00:54:13,819 --> 00:54:16,539
Here. One go for each of them.
Are you drunk?

883
00:54:16,539 --> 00:54:18,259
'Thank you, Jimmy.
How kind of you.'

884
00:54:18,259 --> 00:54:21,739
It is kind. Thanks very much.
Don't flash your money about.

885
00:54:21,739 --> 00:54:23,979
It's my money.
I'll do what I want with it.

886
00:54:25,779 --> 00:54:28,699
Remember, the ring must go
over the base and lie flat.

887
00:54:35,259 --> 00:54:37,419
Ohhh. Now you.

888
00:54:41,899 --> 00:54:45,579
I don't believe it!
I've never won nothing before!

889
00:54:47,579 --> 00:54:49,619
Don't let it make a gambler of you.

890
00:54:49,619 --> 00:54:52,539
See? I told you they were honest.

891
00:54:53,579 --> 00:54:57,419
(Didn't I say to make the blocks
too wide for the bally rings?)

892
00:55:00,699 --> 00:55:02,779
Thanks for not telling Mr Carson

893
00:55:02,779 --> 00:55:04,939
about us sitting down
in the drawing-room.

894
00:55:04,939 --> 00:55:06,579
We'd not be here now.

895
00:55:06,579 --> 00:55:08,579
Well, don't let me catch you again.

896
00:55:09,579 --> 00:55:14,179
Oh, there's Mrs Crawley
with Dr Clarkson!

897
00:55:19,099 --> 00:55:21,859
This is where I belong. I know it.

898
00:55:21,859 --> 00:55:23,659
What, at a fairground stall?

899
00:55:23,659 --> 00:55:26,819
No, working with food. Cooking.
Preparing.

900
00:55:27,859 --> 00:55:29,899
Don't sound so tragic.

901
00:55:29,899 --> 00:55:32,579
Your time at Downton
won't be wasted.

902
00:55:32,579 --> 00:55:36,219
You know how a great house runs
now. That'll come in handy -

903
00:55:39,059 --> 00:55:41,979
What's the matter?
Nothing.

904
00:55:46,059 --> 00:55:48,059
Well, that was great fun!

905
00:55:49,059 --> 00:55:51,099
With the music and everything.

906
00:55:51,099 --> 00:55:53,139
I'm glad we came.

907
00:55:53,139 --> 00:55:55,179
I'm very glad you came.

908
00:55:55,179 --> 00:55:57,219
Shall we sit down for a bit?

909
00:55:57,219 --> 00:55:59,259
Let me fetch you a drink.

910
00:55:59,259 --> 00:56:01,299
There's something I want to ask you.

911
00:56:01,299 --> 00:56:03,299
Punch?
That would be lovely.

912
00:56:07,779 --> 00:56:09,819
You won't miss service?

913
00:56:09,819 --> 00:56:13,459
Well, I've not been unhappy,
you know. I can't pretend I have.

914
00:56:13,459 --> 00:56:15,459
But taking orders from a husband,

915
00:56:15,459 --> 00:56:18,659
it's got to be better than
from some jumped-up lord or lady.

916
00:56:20,259 --> 00:56:21,859
It's still orders, isn't it?

917
00:56:23,739 --> 00:56:25,979
Did you make this pate, as well?

918
00:56:25,979 --> 00:56:28,499
All with my own fair hand.

919
00:56:28,499 --> 00:56:32,539
Well, fair hand or red flipper,
you're the cook for me.

920
00:56:33,979 --> 00:56:35,979
(FAIRGROUND MUSIC IN DISTANCE)

921
00:56:42,499 --> 00:56:45,739
Where do you think you're going,
my laddo? Get out of my way.

922
00:56:51,459 --> 00:56:53,099
Take him.

923
00:56:57,179 --> 00:56:59,299
Let him go.

924
00:56:59,299 --> 00:57:02,099
And who's gonna make me?
I am.

925
00:57:03,139 --> 00:57:04,779
Leave it, Jimmy!

926
00:57:04,779 --> 00:57:06,579
Run! Run!

927
00:57:11,099 --> 00:57:13,499
Go!

928
00:57:28,819 --> 00:57:30,779
There.
Thank you.

929
00:57:30,779 --> 00:57:33,219
I'm so sorry.
The queue was a mile long.

930
00:57:33,219 --> 00:57:36,219
What was it you wanted to ask me?

931
00:57:36,219 --> 00:57:38,259
Ah.

932
00:57:38,259 --> 00:57:40,579
Well, I'm not sure I have the right.

933
00:57:41,619 --> 00:57:45,059
If you'd like me
to come back to the hospital, I -

934
00:57:45,059 --> 00:57:47,059
No, it's not the hospital.

935
00:57:48,219 --> 00:57:49,819
I'd be interested to know...

936
00:57:50,859 --> 00:57:53,179
..if you've ever thought
of marrying again.

937
00:57:53,179 --> 00:57:56,699
Are you thinking of
getting married, Dr Clarkson?

938
00:57:56,699 --> 00:58:01,379
Because if you are, you're a better
man than I am, Gunga Din.

939
00:58:02,419 --> 00:58:04,019
Why?

940
00:58:04,019 --> 00:58:09,619
Well, with good friends like you,
I enjoy my life as it is,

941
00:58:09,619 --> 00:58:12,579
and I wouldn't want to risk things
by changing it.

942
00:58:15,219 --> 00:58:17,219
Excuse me!

943
00:58:19,139 --> 00:58:21,379
Doctor! Doctor,
you've got to come now!

944
00:58:21,379 --> 00:58:23,219
What is it?
It's Thomas. Please!

945
00:58:28,219 --> 00:58:31,139
Rose, you are not wearing
that dress, and that is final.

946
00:58:31,139 --> 00:58:35,379
Oh! Daddy, please stick up for me.
She looks like a slut.

947
00:58:35,379 --> 00:58:38,819
Heavens! That's not a word
you often hear among the heather.

948
00:58:38,819 --> 00:58:42,099
But Princess Mary has one
just like it. It's the fashion now.

949
00:58:42,099 --> 00:58:45,139
Then it is a mad fashion.
Aunt Violet, tell her.

950
00:58:45,139 --> 00:58:47,099
Oh, my dear, in my time, I wore

951
00:58:47,099 --> 00:58:50,259
the crinoline, the bustle
and the leg-o-mutton sleeve.

952
00:58:50,259 --> 00:58:52,619
I'm not in a strong position
to criticise.

953
00:58:52,619 --> 00:58:55,859
Rose, take Aunt Violet
through to the ballroom.

954
00:58:55,859 --> 00:58:56,899
Now.

955
00:59:03,419 --> 00:59:06,979
Do you know, Rose dear, the first
Ghillies' ball I ever attended

956
00:59:06,979 --> 00:59:10,179
was at Balmoral in 1860.

957
00:59:10,179 --> 00:59:12,219
Yes, I'd not long been married,

958
00:59:12,219 --> 00:59:16,299
and I confess, you know,
I was a little alarmed,

959
00:59:16,299 --> 00:59:20,139
because all the men
were as tight as ticks. (GIGGLES)

960
00:59:20,139 --> 00:59:23,739
Don't blame me
if she is the object of ridicule.

961
00:59:23,739 --> 00:59:26,579
I won't, whatever else
I might blame you for.

962
00:59:26,579 --> 00:59:30,099
You are a fool to indulge her.

963
00:59:30,099 --> 00:59:33,099
Have you never stopped to think -
Will you stop?!

964
00:59:34,139 --> 00:59:36,939
Stop making everyone so unhappy
all the bloody time!

965
00:59:39,299 --> 00:59:42,419
Forgive me.
I think I know where I'm going.

966
00:59:42,419 --> 00:59:44,419
I'll come with you.

967
00:59:49,019 --> 00:59:53,139
Is there any chance of apprehending
these men? Not really.

968
00:59:53,139 --> 00:59:56,539
But why did you get into a fight?
It's not like you.

969
00:59:58,579 --> 01:00:02,859
What have they taken? Every penny
I had, but it wasn't much.

970
01:00:02,859 --> 01:00:05,939
Is anything broken?
I don't think so.

971
01:00:05,939 --> 01:00:08,219
He'll be all right?
We ought to get him home.

972
01:00:08,219 --> 01:00:10,379
I'll fetch the wagonette.
I'll help him.

973
01:00:10,379 --> 01:00:12,379
(GROANS)

974
01:00:12,379 --> 01:00:14,419
Lean on me.

975
01:00:14,419 --> 01:00:17,939
Well done. Up here.

976
01:00:25,059 --> 01:00:27,099
(HUMS)

977
01:00:27,099 --> 01:00:29,099
(BABY CRIES)

978
01:00:37,499 --> 01:00:39,499
(CRYING INTENSIFIES)

979
01:00:39,499 --> 01:00:42,979
Hello? What's the matter
with you, eh?

980
01:00:44,059 --> 01:00:46,059
Where's your na... Ohhh!

981
01:00:47,059 --> 01:00:50,219
Let's have a little chat about it.

982
01:00:50,219 --> 01:00:52,219
Eh? There.

983
01:00:56,019 --> 01:00:58,019
(LIVELY MUSIC
ON FIDDLES AND ACCORDION)

984
01:01:05,779 --> 01:01:07,779
We must all join in.

985
01:01:07,779 --> 01:01:10,299
Not me, milady,
and I have a cast-iron alibi.

986
01:01:10,299 --> 01:01:13,939
I can do an eightsome and a dashing
white sergeant, but that's it.

987
01:01:13,939 --> 01:01:17,779
I'm very good. Hamilton House is my
favourite, but I know most of them.

988
01:01:17,779 --> 01:01:20,859
You won't be doing any tonight.
Spoilsport!

989
01:01:20,859 --> 01:01:22,859
I think Mr Crawley's right, milady.

990
01:01:22,859 --> 01:01:24,939
Will you be staying out of it?

991
01:01:24,939 --> 01:01:26,899
We'll have to see.

992
01:01:32,339 --> 01:01:33,979
O'Brien!

993
01:01:33,979 --> 01:01:38,779
Wilkins has been trying her best
to imitate you. What do you think?

994
01:01:38,779 --> 01:01:41,139
It looks very nice, Your Ladyship.

995
01:01:41,139 --> 01:01:43,139
Yes, well, it's not right yet,

996
01:01:43,139 --> 01:01:45,779
but we're trying our best,
aren't we, Wilkins?

997
01:01:46,779 --> 01:01:48,499
I might go and find a drink.

998
01:01:48,499 --> 01:01:51,779
I'll fetch you one.
There's no need. I'll go.

999
01:01:51,779 --> 01:01:53,859
No. I insist.

1000
01:01:53,859 --> 01:01:55,859
You're the guest.

1001
01:02:16,139 --> 01:02:19,579
You must be careful.
Matthew says it's rather strong.

1002
01:02:19,579 --> 01:02:21,939
I should jolly well hope so.

1003
01:02:21,939 --> 01:02:25,259
Rose's evening had a bumpy start.

1004
01:02:25,259 --> 01:02:27,859
I'm afraid Susan isn't herself.

1005
01:02:27,859 --> 01:02:30,699
But she's absolutely herself.

1006
01:02:30,699 --> 01:02:32,699
That's the problem.

1007
01:02:32,699 --> 01:02:35,339
Oh, dear, poor souls.

1008
01:02:35,339 --> 01:02:39,499
It's bad enough parenting a child
when you like each other.

1009
01:02:39,499 --> 01:02:43,299
Can I trouble you for a drop
of whisky, Mr Nield?

1010
01:02:43,299 --> 01:02:44,979
Certainly, Miss Wilkins.

1011
01:02:44,979 --> 01:02:47,779
But I am surprised if it's for you.

1012
01:02:47,779 --> 01:02:49,779
Oh, no!

1013
01:02:49,779 --> 01:02:51,979
For a guest from the south.

1014
01:02:53,379 --> 01:02:55,379
(APPLAUSE AS DANCE ENDS)

1015
01:02:58,579 --> 01:03:00,579
(DOOR OPENS)

1016
01:03:04,019 --> 01:03:06,059
Oh, you're back, then?

1017
01:03:06,059 --> 01:03:08,059
We are.

1018
01:03:09,059 --> 01:03:11,579
And we've a few stories to tell.

1019
01:03:11,579 --> 01:03:15,339
But you've spent your day
more productively, I see.

1020
01:03:17,379 --> 01:03:19,419
Where's Nanny?
I'm not sure,

1021
01:03:19,419 --> 01:03:22,339
but she'll find us in a moment.

1022
01:03:22,339 --> 01:03:25,619
I was thinking about Lady Sybil
when she was this age.

1023
01:03:25,619 --> 01:03:29,619
All we can do for her now
is to cherish her bairn.

1024
01:03:29,619 --> 01:03:32,979
And it's lovely
to watch you doing just that.

1025
01:03:32,979 --> 01:03:35,859
There's no need to get sentimental,
Mrs Hughes.

1026
01:03:37,579 --> 01:03:39,859
Right. Let's get this one
back to bed.

1027
01:03:39,859 --> 01:03:42,819
Come on.
Shall we go and look for Nanny?

1028
01:03:55,219 --> 01:03:56,859
Where is Shrimpie?

1029
01:03:56,859 --> 01:03:59,979
Hiding and licking his wounds.
I'll go in search of him,

1030
01:03:59,979 --> 01:04:02,939
poor fellow,
if he hasn't come back.

1031
01:04:04,939 --> 01:04:06,939
That's very kind. Thank you.

1032
01:04:16,579 --> 01:04:18,579
Don't you want it?
No.

1033
01:04:19,619 --> 01:04:21,739
I wouldn't drink that
if I were you, Mr -

1034
01:04:23,379 --> 01:04:26,259
Oh, that slipped down a treat!

1035
01:04:26,259 --> 01:04:28,259
I'll see if I can get another one.

1036
01:04:29,299 --> 01:04:31,019
Each to his own.

1037
01:04:31,979 --> 01:04:34,499
Was it always this bad?

1038
01:04:34,499 --> 01:04:36,499
Not at the beginning.

1039
01:04:36,499 --> 01:04:38,579
We weren't madly in love,

1040
01:04:38,579 --> 01:04:42,659
but there was a job to be done,
and we both believed in it.

1041
01:04:43,979 --> 01:04:46,659
(SIGHS)
Then the children came along,

1042
01:04:46,659 --> 01:04:50,499
and for years
we hardly had time to think.

1043
01:04:50,499 --> 01:04:52,219
What went wrong?

1044
01:04:52,219 --> 01:04:56,499
First James left,
then Annabel got married.

1045
01:04:58,059 --> 01:05:01,899
We started to learn
just how little we have in common.

1046
01:05:09,059 --> 01:05:11,059
Why is Susan so hard on Rose?

1047
01:05:11,059 --> 01:05:13,339
Who knows?

1048
01:05:13,339 --> 01:05:17,379
Perhaps Rose reminds her
of me as I used to be.

1049
01:05:18,419 --> 01:05:23,059
Me, when I had
something to live for.

1050
01:05:23,059 --> 01:05:25,619
Oh, you have a great deal
to live for.

1051
01:05:25,619 --> 01:05:29,059
Duneagle!
I can't remember being as envious

1052
01:05:29,059 --> 01:05:31,059
as I have been these past few days.

1053
01:05:31,059 --> 01:05:33,979
Don't be. It'll all have to go.

1054
01:05:34,979 --> 01:05:36,979
What?

1055
01:05:38,059 --> 01:05:40,099
It's my own fault.

1056
01:05:40,099 --> 01:05:43,499
If I'd had any gumption
and modernised, as you did...

1057
01:05:43,499 --> 01:05:47,339
But I sat on my hands
as the money drained away.

1058
01:05:48,339 --> 01:05:50,339
Now it's all gone.

1059
01:05:53,299 --> 01:05:55,339
What will you do?

1060
01:05:55,339 --> 01:05:57,659
Go to India first, and then London.

1061
01:05:57,659 --> 01:05:59,699
Oh, it'll be all right.

1062
01:05:59,699 --> 01:06:02,019
What with the club and the Lords,

1063
01:06:02,019 --> 01:06:05,579
Susan and I needn't see
too much of each other.

1064
01:06:09,139 --> 01:06:11,659
Shrimpie, my dear chap.
I'm so sorry.

1065
01:06:13,459 --> 01:06:16,499
Question is: Rose.

1066
01:06:18,899 --> 01:06:21,299
What are we going to do about Rose?

1067
01:06:27,040 --> 01:06:29,040
(LIVELY MUSIC)

1068
01:06:31,640 --> 01:06:35,080
Anna! Come on. This is it.

1069
01:06:35,080 --> 01:06:37,200
This is what?
You'll see! Quick! Come on!

1070
01:06:47,320 --> 01:06:50,480
Look at Anna.
She never said she could reel!

1071
01:06:51,520 --> 01:06:53,560
Bates, did you know?

1072
01:06:53,560 --> 01:06:55,560
No, milady. I never knew.

1073
01:06:57,480 --> 01:06:59,480
But isn't she marvellous?

1074
01:07:02,800 --> 01:07:04,840
Yes.

1075
01:07:04,840 --> 01:07:06,840
She is marvellous.

1076
01:07:21,480 --> 01:07:24,400
Oh, Mrs Patmore. How kind of you.

1077
01:07:24,400 --> 01:07:28,400
I want a word, so I thought we
could have some tea while I get it.

1078
01:07:31,080 --> 01:07:33,000
You were right.

1079
01:07:33,000 --> 01:07:35,640
He says he loves me
and he can't live without me.

1080
01:07:35,640 --> 01:07:37,680
(POURS TEA)

1081
01:07:37,680 --> 01:07:39,720
Oh, dear.

1082
01:07:39,720 --> 01:07:41,800
What do you mean, 'Oh, dear'?

1083
01:07:41,800 --> 01:07:46,440
It's a long time since anyone
wanted to share my seat on the bus,
never mind my heart and home.

1084
01:07:47,480 --> 01:07:49,520
I don't know how to say it.

1085
01:07:49,520 --> 01:07:51,520
Well, find a way.

1086
01:07:55,520 --> 01:08:00,800
Well, I first noticed it when
I was standing at the stall.

1087
01:08:02,560 --> 01:08:04,920
He was flirting with
that young assistant

1088
01:08:04,920 --> 01:08:07,880
and stroking her...
well, bottom.

1089
01:08:07,880 --> 01:08:09,920
What?
It's true.

1090
01:08:09,920 --> 01:08:12,480
Then I saw him making eyes
at some other women

1091
01:08:12,480 --> 01:08:15,120
when he was pulling
in the tug-of-war.

1092
01:08:15,120 --> 01:08:17,960
While I was cheering him?
Later, when I was with Alfred,

1093
01:08:17,960 --> 01:08:20,400
I saw Mr Tufton in the crowd,

1094
01:08:20,400 --> 01:08:25,400
and he seemed to be chewing
the mouth off some poor woman.

1095
01:08:26,440 --> 01:08:28,480
Where was I all this time?

1096
01:08:28,480 --> 01:08:30,120
I don't know.

1097
01:08:30,120 --> 01:08:32,520
Dreaming of a better life?

1098
01:08:34,960 --> 01:08:36,960
Oh, Mrs Patmore, I am sorry.

1099
01:08:37,960 --> 01:08:41,000
I don't know
if he wanted to eat a few dinners

1100
01:08:41,000 --> 01:08:43,040
before he told you the truth,

1101
01:08:43,040 --> 01:08:46,080
or if he planned to marry you
and chain you to the stove.

1102
01:08:48,160 --> 01:08:50,800
Either way,
it was the cooking he was after...

1103
01:08:53,280 --> 01:08:54,920
..and not me.

1104
01:08:57,040 --> 01:08:59,080
I feel terrible.

1105
01:08:59,080 --> 01:09:02,480
I should have pulled you away
then and there,

1106
01:09:02,480 --> 01:09:05,120
but you were having
such a good time.

1107
01:09:10,000 --> 01:09:11,840
Is there anything I can do?

1108
01:09:11,840 --> 01:09:13,160
No.

1109
01:09:14,760 --> 01:09:17,320
God, I've never felt more relieved
in all my life!

1110
01:09:18,560 --> 01:09:20,000
What?

1111
01:09:20,000 --> 01:09:24,840
The more he said about how he liked
his beef roasted, his eggs fried

1112
01:09:24,840 --> 01:09:29,120
and his pancakes flipped, the more
I wondered how to get away.

1113
01:09:30,920 --> 01:09:32,720
And what if he comes back?

1114
01:09:32,720 --> 01:09:34,880
He'll get a thick ear,
and no mistake.

1115
01:09:36,440 --> 01:09:38,880
But how could he do such a thing,
Mrs Hughes?

1116
01:09:38,880 --> 01:09:41,400
How could he lead a poor woman on
like that?

1117
01:09:41,400 --> 01:09:43,440
(SIGHS)

1118
01:09:43,440 --> 01:09:47,040
You heard him, Mrs Patmore.
Any time, any place -

1119
01:09:47,040 --> 01:09:50,080
he loves to be in love.

1120
01:09:52,240 --> 01:09:54,240
(BOTH GIGGLE)

1121
01:09:57,320 --> 01:09:59,680
And that was really his reaction?

1122
01:09:59,680 --> 01:10:01,720
How disappointing.

1123
01:10:01,720 --> 01:10:04,560
Well, I wasn't going to tell you
till I was leaving.

1124
01:10:04,560 --> 01:10:06,600
Why not?

1125
01:10:06,600 --> 01:10:08,640
Because I... I...

1126
01:10:08,640 --> 01:10:12,320
I wanted us to have
a last evening together.

1127
01:10:13,880 --> 01:10:16,440
This is not our last evening.

1128
01:10:17,480 --> 01:10:19,760
Isn't it?
It's odd.

1129
01:10:19,760 --> 01:10:23,200
If you'd asked me before tonight,
how I felt about you,

1130
01:10:23,200 --> 01:10:25,960
I'm not sure
what I would have answered.

1131
01:10:25,960 --> 01:10:31,240
But now I'm absolutely sure,
and this is not our last evening.

1132
01:10:31,240 --> 01:10:33,240
(BAND STRIKES UP)

1133
01:10:38,440 --> 01:10:40,080
Ha-ha!

1134
01:10:43,800 --> 01:10:45,120
(WHOOPS)

1135
01:10:45,120 --> 01:10:47,200
What in God's name...

1136
01:10:48,600 --> 01:10:51,120
(LAUGHS HYSTERICALLY)

1137
01:10:52,160 --> 01:10:54,480
Are you proud of your handiwork?

1138
01:10:54,480 --> 01:10:56,720
I don't know what you mean.

1139
01:10:56,720 --> 01:10:59,000
Never mind, Miss Wilkins.

1140
01:10:59,000 --> 01:11:02,120
It might do him good
to let it all go for once.

1141
01:11:02,120 --> 01:11:04,800
And I'm grateful. I am.

1142
01:11:04,800 --> 01:11:06,680
What for?

1143
01:11:06,680 --> 01:11:10,960
Because I need never be held back
by any sense of loyalty to you.

1144
01:11:16,080 --> 01:11:18,360
(MOLESLEY LAUGHS HYSTERICALLY)

1145
01:11:23,600 --> 01:11:27,240
They do say there's a wild man
inside all of us.

1146
01:11:27,240 --> 01:11:29,920
If only he would stay inside.

1147
01:11:41,320 --> 01:11:45,840
I thought you'd like to know
Mr Barrow is feeling much better.

1148
01:11:47,400 --> 01:11:49,680
Thank you, but you should go now.

1149
01:11:49,680 --> 01:11:54,040
Just wanted to tell you
what a lovely day I've had.

1150
01:11:54,040 --> 01:11:56,600
Really lovely.

1151
01:11:56,600 --> 01:12:00,280
Do you want to meet for lunch
tomorrow in the Grantham Arms?

1152
01:12:48,760 --> 01:12:50,800
Are you all right?

1153
01:12:50,800 --> 01:12:52,800
(DANCE ENDS)

1154
01:13:00,000 --> 01:13:03,280
I don't think I should have done
that, but I couldn't resist.

1155
01:13:03,280 --> 01:13:06,840
This is just what I was afraid of.
Calm down. Everything's fine.

1156
01:13:06,840 --> 01:13:10,640
But I wonder, would you mind
terribly if I went home tomorrow?

1157
01:13:10,640 --> 01:13:13,880
Of course not.
I'll tell Shrimpie tonight.

1158
01:13:13,880 --> 01:13:15,920
No, not you. You must stay here.

1159
01:13:15,920 --> 01:13:18,360
What? I don't want to let you
out of my sight.

1160
01:13:18,360 --> 01:13:21,880
If you come, Mama and Papa will
leave, and the party will break up.

1161
01:13:21,880 --> 01:13:24,120
So what? I'm coming with you.

1162
01:13:24,120 --> 01:13:27,160
It's unfair on Susan and Shrimpie.
I'm perfectly all right.

1163
01:13:27,160 --> 01:13:29,800
It's only a couple more days.

1164
01:13:29,800 --> 01:13:32,040
Let me go,
and I'll see you when I see you.

1165
01:13:32,040 --> 01:13:33,200
Please.

1166
01:13:35,280 --> 01:13:39,960
Besides, Molesley may need a bit
of time before he's fit to travel.

1167
01:13:39,960 --> 01:13:41,920
(SNORES)

1168
01:13:45,360 --> 01:13:47,400
That was Mr Bates on the telephone.

1169
01:13:47,400 --> 01:13:49,680
Lady Mary and Anna
are coming back today.

1170
01:13:49,680 --> 01:13:51,400
They're already on the train.

1171
01:13:51,400 --> 01:13:55,040
Well, we'd better look sharp. Edna,
air the room and make up the bed

1172
01:13:55,040 --> 01:13:57,040
while I go and talk to Mrs Patmore.

1173
01:13:57,040 --> 01:13:59,080
Must I?

1174
01:13:59,080 --> 01:14:01,120
Why?

1175
01:14:01,120 --> 01:14:03,200
Do you have other plans, Edna?

1176
01:14:04,280 --> 01:14:08,120
I said I'd meet...Tom Branson
for lunch in the village.

1177
01:14:08,120 --> 01:14:10,160
Did you, indeed?

1178
01:14:10,160 --> 01:14:12,160
'Tom' Branson?

1179
01:14:17,680 --> 01:14:20,520
Before you start,
it may not be his fault.

1180
01:14:20,520 --> 01:14:23,520
Whether it's his fault or not,
she has to go.

1181
01:14:23,520 --> 01:14:25,160
Oh, yes.

1182
01:14:25,160 --> 01:14:27,800
Of course she has to go.

1183
01:14:28,840 --> 01:14:30,880
Will you tell him, or will I?

1184
01:14:30,880 --> 01:14:34,480
You'd better do it. I'd only be rude,
which wouldn't help anyone.

1185
01:14:42,360 --> 01:14:44,000
You've heard about Mary?

1186
01:14:44,000 --> 01:14:46,480
I have. Her maid left a message
with O'Brien.

1187
01:14:46,480 --> 01:14:49,520
I hope you don't think her rude.

1188
01:14:49,520 --> 01:14:51,960
I know you haven't always
approved of Mary.

1189
01:14:51,960 --> 01:14:54,000
She's having a baby.

1190
01:14:54,000 --> 01:14:57,800
We all need a little leeway
when it comes to our babies.

1191
01:14:57,800 --> 01:15:02,240
And as for the other business,
I'm not as harsh as I was.

1192
01:15:04,080 --> 01:15:07,040
Rose is proving quite an education.

1193
01:15:07,040 --> 01:15:09,200
I can imagine.

1194
01:15:09,200 --> 01:15:11,560
I find myself worrying about Rose

1195
01:15:11,560 --> 01:15:14,440
before I even open my eyes
for the morning.

1196
01:15:16,200 --> 01:15:18,320
Do you think I'm being stupid?

1197
01:15:18,320 --> 01:15:21,840
Not at all. I understand better
than anyone else here could.

1198
01:15:22,880 --> 01:15:26,400
Shrimpie wants her to live
at Downton while we're in India.

1199
01:15:26,400 --> 01:15:30,040
I've told Robert I would never
agree to that against your wishes.

1200
01:15:30,040 --> 01:15:32,400
I know. Thank you.

1201
01:15:32,400 --> 01:15:35,400
It's not often that I get support
in this house.

1202
01:15:37,160 --> 01:15:40,040
But I wonder now if he isn't right,

1203
01:15:40,040 --> 01:15:43,520
and that we need a rest
from one another.

1204
01:15:43,520 --> 01:15:47,840
Apart from anything else,
I can't bring her out from Bombay.

1205
01:15:48,920 --> 01:15:51,000
But would you be prepared
for all that?

1206
01:15:51,000 --> 01:15:53,960
If you want me to be,
and only if you want it.

1207
01:15:55,560 --> 01:15:57,720
What about you and Shrimpie?

1208
01:15:57,720 --> 01:15:59,760
Oh, we'll soldier on.

1209
01:15:59,760 --> 01:16:01,760
Our sort never accept defeat.

1210
01:16:04,920 --> 01:16:07,000
Even if I wish we could.

1211
01:16:13,280 --> 01:16:16,200
Will you speak well of me to her?

1212
01:16:16,200 --> 01:16:20,320
Not every day, but sometimes?

1213
01:16:20,320 --> 01:16:22,320
Course I will. I promise.

1214
01:16:27,080 --> 01:16:29,120
So I spoiled things for her.

1215
01:16:29,120 --> 01:16:33,080
I'm afraid the work
would no longer satisfy her.

1216
01:16:33,080 --> 01:16:35,120
I've seen it before.

1217
01:16:35,120 --> 01:16:37,560
She'd unsettle the other maids.

1218
01:16:37,560 --> 01:16:39,600
I didn't encourage her, you know.

1219
01:16:39,600 --> 01:16:43,400
Maybe. But if I may say it,
you didn't discourage her, either.

1220
01:16:45,800 --> 01:16:47,800
Can I ask one thing?

1221
01:16:49,320 --> 01:16:52,200
That you give her
a decent reference, please.

1222
01:16:52,200 --> 01:16:56,560
I will. Though I don't think
she's cut out to be a housemaid.

1223
01:17:00,400 --> 01:17:04,680
Would you allow me to speak
as I would have in the old days?

1224
01:17:04,680 --> 01:17:06,680
Go on, then.

1225
01:17:08,400 --> 01:17:11,760
You let Edna make you ashamed
of your new life.

1226
01:17:12,800 --> 01:17:14,800
But you've done well.

1227
01:17:15,880 --> 01:17:18,760
And Lady Sybil would be so proud.

1228
01:17:27,800 --> 01:17:29,800
I can't bear to be without her.

1229
01:17:35,600 --> 01:17:37,600
(SOBS)

1230
01:17:39,320 --> 01:17:41,360
You must bear it.

1231
01:17:41,360 --> 01:17:44,760
And one day I hope,
and so would she,

1232
01:17:44,760 --> 01:17:47,000
you'll find someone
to bear it with you.

1233
01:17:47,000 --> 01:17:52,160
But until then, be your own master
and call your own tune.

1234
01:18:01,020 --> 01:18:03,020
Thank you.

1235
01:18:11,060 --> 01:18:14,220
Milady? Is something the matter?

1236
01:18:14,220 --> 01:18:17,140
I don't want to alarm anyone,
but will you leave the cases

1237
01:18:17,140 --> 01:18:19,300
and take me
straight to the hospital?

1238
01:18:19,300 --> 01:18:20,900
Milady.
What?

1239
01:18:20,900 --> 01:18:24,620
Let Mrs Crawley know, and get a
message to Mr Crawley straight away.

1240
01:18:35,820 --> 01:18:37,420
Mr Crawley?

1241
01:18:38,420 --> 01:18:40,460
(SIGHS) Mr Crawley!

1242
01:18:40,460 --> 01:18:42,380
What in God's name...?

1243
01:18:52,500 --> 01:18:56,820
Right. All change. The whole family
is coming back tomorrow

1244
01:18:56,820 --> 01:18:58,540
and we must be ready to receive them.

1245
01:18:58,540 --> 01:19:01,780
Is it because Lady Mary's
in the hospital? It is.

1246
01:19:01,780 --> 01:19:05,700
Does that mean she's in danger?
No. It doesn't mean any such thing.

1247
01:19:05,700 --> 01:19:10,180
Lady Mary will be perfectly fine,
but we have to make allowances.

1248
01:19:15,460 --> 01:19:18,060
Now, do you have everything?

1249
01:19:21,100 --> 01:19:22,900
What have I done wrong?

1250
01:19:23,940 --> 01:19:25,980
I'm as good as Mr Branson.

1251
01:19:25,980 --> 01:19:30,180
And there was nothing improper.
Nothing at all.

1252
01:19:30,180 --> 01:19:32,180
I'm sure.

1253
01:19:33,580 --> 01:19:36,060
But there are rules
to this way of life, Edna.

1254
01:19:36,060 --> 01:19:38,420
And if you're not prepared
to live by them,

1255
01:19:38,420 --> 01:19:41,220
then it's not the right life
for you.

1256
01:19:52,740 --> 01:19:56,620
It's organised. We've got tickets
for the first train in the morning.

1257
01:19:56,620 --> 01:20:00,180
There isn't one before then.
I just wish I was there with her.

1258
01:20:03,940 --> 01:20:05,940
I can't wait to get home.

1259
01:20:07,220 --> 01:20:09,820
Aren't you enjoying your Victorian
idyll any longer?

1260
01:20:09,820 --> 01:20:11,740
I'm glad I was jealous of Shrimpie.

1261
01:20:11,740 --> 01:20:13,860
It's made me realise
what a fool I've been.

1262
01:20:13,860 --> 01:20:16,380
Downton will survive
because of Matthew's vision.

1263
01:20:16,380 --> 01:20:18,420
I'm so pleased to hear you say it.

1264
01:20:18,420 --> 01:20:20,860
You always knew
how lucky we are in Matthew,

1265
01:20:20,860 --> 01:20:22,780
and now I give thanks for him

1266
01:20:22,780 --> 01:20:25,620
as I give thanks
for my home and my family.

1267
01:20:25,620 --> 01:20:28,140
And most of all,
I give thanks for my wife.

1268
01:20:33,580 --> 01:20:35,660
Send us the news
as soon as you know it.

1269
01:20:35,660 --> 01:20:38,660
And thank you for taking in Rose.

1270
01:20:38,660 --> 01:20:41,700
We'll make firm plans
when we know when we're leaving.

1271
01:20:41,700 --> 01:20:44,940
I'm glad to have her,
now I know it's what you both want.

1272
01:20:46,060 --> 01:20:50,700
What I want is for her to know that
family can be a loving thing.

1273
01:20:50,700 --> 01:20:52,740
We'll do our best.

1274
01:20:52,740 --> 01:20:55,300
Love is like riding,
or speaking French.

1275
01:20:56,780 --> 01:20:59,260
If you don't learn it young,

1276
01:20:59,260 --> 01:21:01,340
it's hard to get
the trick of it later.

1277
01:21:01,340 --> 01:21:04,020
Well said, Shrimpie. And good luck.

1278
01:21:09,740 --> 01:21:12,300
What did you tell Mary
about Michael Gregson?

1279
01:21:12,300 --> 01:21:13,620
Nothing.

1280
01:21:13,620 --> 01:21:15,900
I hope you made it clear
what has to happen.

1281
01:21:15,900 --> 01:21:18,740
Oh, yes. We both know
what happens next.

1282
01:21:19,900 --> 01:21:23,500
I'll see you at Downton very soon
and thank you both so much.

1283
01:21:23,500 --> 01:21:25,540
We look forward to it.

1284
01:21:25,540 --> 01:21:29,020
Are we all ready, Bates?
We are, my lord.

1285
01:21:33,380 --> 01:21:35,380
(ENGINE STARTS UP)

1286
01:21:54,140 --> 01:21:56,500
Mr Carson? I'm going down
to the hospital,

1287
01:21:56,500 --> 01:21:58,540
and I think I have what she needs.

1288
01:21:58,540 --> 01:22:01,300
If there's anything else at all,
just telephone.

1289
01:22:01,300 --> 01:22:04,740
According to the doctor,
there's nothing to worry about.

1290
01:22:04,740 --> 01:22:08,780
Of course I worry. After Lady Sybil,
how could I not worry?

1291
01:22:08,780 --> 01:22:11,780
Now, should I meet the others
off the train?

1292
01:22:11,780 --> 01:22:14,140
Get Mr Matthew's car
taken to the station,

1293
01:22:14,140 --> 01:22:16,180
and have the others brought here.

1294
01:22:16,180 --> 01:22:18,300
Mr Matthew can drive himself
to the hospital

1295
01:22:18,300 --> 01:22:20,420
and come back with the news
when he's ready.

1296
01:22:20,420 --> 01:22:22,340
Yes, good. Very good.

1297
01:22:22,340 --> 01:22:25,180
And, Anna, thank you.

1298
01:22:32,380 --> 01:22:36,340
Would you help to prepare her while
I make sure everything's ready?

1299
01:22:36,340 --> 01:22:41,260
Erm, you saved me from making
a fool of myself at the fair.

1300
01:22:41,260 --> 01:22:43,580
I'm afraid I'd too much to drink.

1301
01:22:43,580 --> 01:22:45,180
I don't know what you mean.

1302
01:22:46,220 --> 01:22:48,260
Well, I think you do.

1303
01:22:48,260 --> 01:22:50,420
And thank you.

1304
01:22:57,980 --> 01:22:59,620
I wish Matthew were here.

1305
01:22:59,620 --> 01:23:03,660
It's funny. I feel as if
I'm only half myself without him.

1306
01:23:03,660 --> 01:23:05,380
He'll be on the train by now,

1307
01:23:05,380 --> 01:23:09,140
and you won't want him in the room
till it's all over, trust me!

1308
01:23:09,140 --> 01:23:11,180
Oh!

1309
01:23:11,180 --> 01:23:13,180
It won't be long now.

1310
01:23:14,740 --> 01:23:19,260
We must ring Carson.
He'll be in such a state. I will.

1311
01:23:19,260 --> 01:23:22,340
I shouldn't have gone up north.
How could I be so stupid?

1312
01:23:22,340 --> 01:23:24,740
My dear,
the baby will be perfectly well.

1313
01:23:24,740 --> 01:23:26,580
Slightly early, but not very.

1314
01:23:26,580 --> 01:23:29,260
We'll just have to take
a little extra care, that's all.

1315
01:23:29,260 --> 01:23:30,660
(KNOCKING)

1316
01:23:49,780 --> 01:23:51,780
(KNOCK AT DOOR)

1317
01:23:59,820 --> 01:24:01,540
What are you doing up here?

1318
01:24:04,900 --> 01:24:07,780
I just wanted to make sure there
wasn't too much harm done.

1319
01:24:07,780 --> 01:24:10,900
Well, there was enough harm done.

1320
01:24:12,940 --> 01:24:15,020
You were brave, Mr Barrow.

1321
01:24:17,420 --> 01:24:19,020
Very brave.

1322
01:24:21,100 --> 01:24:23,140
I feel badly.

1323
01:24:23,140 --> 01:24:24,980
I shouldn't have run off.

1324
01:24:24,980 --> 01:24:27,020
Oh, you should have.

1325
01:24:27,020 --> 01:24:29,340
Otherwise, what was I
bloody doing it for?

1326
01:24:34,940 --> 01:24:37,020
Were you following me?

1327
01:24:40,380 --> 01:24:42,380
I had to keep an eye out.

1328
01:24:44,140 --> 01:24:46,180
I could see
you'd had a bit to drink.

1329
01:24:46,180 --> 01:24:48,180
So...

1330
01:24:51,180 --> 01:24:53,220
..yes.

1331
01:24:53,220 --> 01:24:55,380
Yes, I did follow you.

1332
01:24:55,380 --> 01:24:57,300
Why?

1333
01:24:57,300 --> 01:24:59,220
You know why.

1334
01:25:17,220 --> 01:25:19,380
I can never give you what you want.

1335
01:25:19,380 --> 01:25:21,980
I understand that. I do.

1336
01:25:21,980 --> 01:25:24,460
And I don't ask for it.

1337
01:25:26,100 --> 01:25:28,900
But I'd like it
if we could be friends.

1338
01:25:32,820 --> 01:25:34,980
Right you are, Mr Barrow.

1339
01:25:34,980 --> 01:25:38,820
If that's all,
I think I could manage that.

1340
01:25:39,860 --> 01:25:41,580
Thank you, Jimmy.

1341
01:25:41,580 --> 01:25:43,620
Thank you.

1342
01:25:43,620 --> 01:25:46,540
Make yourself useful for
something, read the paper.

1343
01:25:47,700 --> 01:25:50,500
4:20 at York looks all right
for a flutter.

1344
01:25:53,140 --> 01:25:54,980
Thank you.

1345
01:25:54,980 --> 01:25:57,220
Thank you very much.
We'll keep you posted.

1346
01:25:57,220 --> 01:25:59,220
Thank you very much indeed.

1347
01:26:06,220 --> 01:26:08,700
What about jelly?
That was Mrs Crawley.

1348
01:26:08,700 --> 01:26:12,140
It's over. Lady Mary's very tired,
but she's come through it.

1349
01:26:12,140 --> 01:26:15,460
They both have.
Thank the Lord!

1350
01:26:15,460 --> 01:26:17,340
What about the baby?

1351
01:26:17,340 --> 01:26:19,940
What about it?
What sex is it?

1352
01:26:19,940 --> 01:26:22,220
Oh! I never thought to ask.

1353
01:26:22,220 --> 01:26:24,220
Oh, God. Men!

1354
01:26:31,420 --> 01:26:34,300
Can this hot and dusty traveller
come in?

1355
01:26:41,300 --> 01:26:43,300
Say hello to your son and heir.

1356
01:26:57,340 --> 01:27:00,060
Hello, my dearest little chap.

1357
01:27:05,460 --> 01:27:09,260
I wonder if he has any idea
how much joy he brings with him.

1358
01:27:14,620 --> 01:27:16,660
Oh, my darling. How are you?

1359
01:27:16,660 --> 01:27:18,140
Really?

1360
01:27:18,140 --> 01:27:21,820
Tired, and pretty relieved.

1361
01:27:22,980 --> 01:27:26,460
But just think,
we've done our duty.

1362
01:27:26,460 --> 01:27:28,500
Downton is safe.

1363
01:27:28,500 --> 01:27:31,460
Papa must be dancing a jig.

1364
01:27:31,460 --> 01:27:33,500
I'm dancing a jig!

1365
01:27:33,500 --> 01:27:36,220
I feel like I swallowed
a box of fireworks.

1366
01:27:50,980 --> 01:27:53,420
You are going to be
such a wonderful mother.

1367
01:27:53,420 --> 01:27:55,420
How do you know?

1368
01:27:56,620 --> 01:27:58,620
Because...

1369
01:28:00,740 --> 01:28:02,940
Because you're such
a wonderful woman.

1370
01:28:06,820 --> 01:28:11,060
I hope I'm allowed to be
your Mary Crawley for all eternity

1371
01:28:11,060 --> 01:28:14,980
and not Edith's version,
or anyone else's for that matter.

1372
01:28:15,980 --> 01:28:17,980
You'll be my Mary always.

1373
01:28:19,020 --> 01:28:21,020
Cos mine is the true Mary.

1374
01:28:23,500 --> 01:28:25,900
Do you ever wonder
how happy you've made me?

1375
01:28:27,620 --> 01:28:29,660
You sound rather foreign.

1376
01:28:29,660 --> 01:28:34,180
Shouldn't you be saying, 'You'll be
up and about in no time'?

1377
01:28:35,220 --> 01:28:37,260
I'll do all that tomorrow.

1378
01:28:38,380 --> 01:28:40,940
But, right now, I want to tell you

1379
01:28:40,940 --> 01:28:43,940
that I fall more in love with you
every day that passes.

1380
01:28:45,860 --> 01:28:48,660
I'll remind you of that
next time I scratch the car.

1381
01:28:48,660 --> 01:28:50,740
Do.

1382
01:28:50,740 --> 01:28:52,740
I give you full permission.

1383
01:28:55,660 --> 01:28:57,740
Where are the others?

1384
01:28:57,740 --> 01:29:01,780
Back at the house,
panting to see you.

1385
01:29:01,780 --> 01:29:03,820
To see you both.

1386
01:29:03,820 --> 01:29:06,660
But I've sent Mother
to keep them at bay.

1387
01:29:08,420 --> 01:29:10,420
I wanted the chance to be alone...

1388
01:29:11,660 --> 01:29:13,700
..with my family.

1389
01:29:15,660 --> 01:29:17,660
You'd better go and tell them.

1390
01:29:18,740 --> 01:29:22,180
But first, I think I've earned
a decent kiss.

1391
01:29:23,220 --> 01:29:25,820
You certainly, certainly have.

1392
01:29:39,100 --> 01:29:42,100
LADY EDITH:
First Sibby, now her little cousin.

1393
01:29:42,100 --> 01:29:45,500
It's rather wonderful, the way
families just keep on rolling.

1394
01:29:45,500 --> 01:29:47,580
Did you have a good time?
I did.

1395
01:29:47,580 --> 01:29:49,580
Very good, in a way.

1396
01:29:49,580 --> 01:29:51,660
How about you?

1397
01:29:51,660 --> 01:29:53,740
I've been on
a bit of a learning curve.

1398
01:29:53,740 --> 01:29:56,580
Me too, and it isn't over yet.

1399
01:29:56,580 --> 01:29:58,940
These things
are always nerve-wracking.

1400
01:29:58,940 --> 01:30:01,300
All's well that ends well,
and it won't be long

1401
01:30:01,300 --> 01:30:06,300
before you'll be able to say
hello to your very own grandson.

1402
01:30:06,300 --> 01:30:08,380
My grandson!

1403
01:30:08,380 --> 01:30:11,940
Oh, my dear! How sweet
and miraculous that sounds!

1404
01:30:24,940 --> 01:30:27,180
Life is strange, isn't it?

1405
01:30:27,180 --> 01:30:29,740
In so many different ways.

1406
01:30:29,740 --> 01:30:31,780
No, I mean,

1407
01:30:31,780 --> 01:30:34,940
I think of all the uncertainty
between Matthew and Mary,

1408
01:30:34,940 --> 01:30:39,820
or when the money was lost,
and everything was so dark.

1409
01:30:39,820 --> 01:30:42,820
Yet now here we are
with two healthy heirs,

1410
01:30:42,820 --> 01:30:44,860
an estate in good order,

1411
01:30:44,860 --> 01:30:48,140
and I wonder what I've done
to deserve it?

1412
01:30:51,300 --> 01:30:53,140
I agree.

1413
01:30:53,140 --> 01:30:57,460
But then, we don't always
get our just desserts.

1414
01:31:12,660 --> 01:31:15,380
You'd better go down.
They'll be here in a minute.

1415
01:31:17,580 --> 01:31:20,140
And tell Mr Matthew
he must wait his turn.

1416
01:31:20,140 --> 01:31:24,260
He's seen the baby,
and they haven't.

